// Generated by authoring/psyc301/build_qa_v5.py from authoring/psyc301/qa-v5/*.py
window.QA_V5 = [{"id": 0, "a": ["**Memory** is the set of processes that let us retain, retrieve and use information about stimuli, images, events, ideas and skills after the original information is no longer present. The experience is over, but something from it is still available to work with. A past experience that shapes what someone thinks or does now, even though nothing in the surroundings supplies it, is the clearest sign that memory is at work."]}, {"id": 1, "a": ["**Endel Tulving** described **episodic memory** as a kind of **mental time travel**. When you remember a particular event, you connect the present back to a moment you actually experienced, and that connection is what makes it an episode rather than a general fact."]}, {"id": 2, "a": ["**Encoding**, **storage** and **retrieval** are three separate operations. Encoding converts information into a usable form so it can be stored, storage retains it, and retrieval makes it available again when it is needed. Because they are separate steps, a failure to use information can come from any of them."]}, {"id": 3, "a": ["Memory is **not an unchanged copy** of experience. It is an **active system** that can organize and alter information, which is why what you remember can differ from what you first experienced."]}, {"id": 4, "a": ["Because three operations could each fail, forgetting by itself does not show which one broke down. The information may never have been encoded well, may have been lost from storage, or may be stored but not retrievable at that moment. Telling these apart takes more than noticing that something was not remembered."]}, {"id": 5, "a": ["The **memory tree** divides memory into **sensory**, **short-term** and **long-term** memory. Long-term memory then branches into **declarative memory**, which holds episodic events and semantic knowledge, and **procedural memory**, which concerns skills. The tree sorts memories by what they do rather than by how long they last."]}, {"id": 6, "a": ["Long-term memory is not one uniform store. Declarative memory holds events and facts, and procedural memory holds skills, so knowing a fact and knowing how to do something are stored in different branches."]}, {"id": 7, "a": ["**Episodic** and **semantic** memory are both branches of **declarative memory**, which sits inside long-term memory. They are not additional short-term stores. Episodic memory holds events, and semantic memory holds general knowledge."]}, {"id": 8, "a": ["**Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968) modal model** divides memory into **sensory memory**, **short-term memory** and **long-term memory**. Information moves through them in that order, and attention and rehearsal, the control processes, decide what is carried forward."]}, {"id": 9, "a": ["The three stores differ in how long they hold information and how much. **Sensory memory** briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second. **Short-term memory** holds five to seven items for about 15 to 20 seconds. **Long-term memory** retains large amounts for years or decades, so it is the only one of the three described as lasting years or decades."]}, {"id": 10, "a": ["**Rehearsal** is a control process, something the person does with information rather than a store. For instance, repeating a phone number keeps it available and can support its transfer onward. Repetition is therefore what lets information last."]}, {"id": 11, "a": ["Looking at a **phone directory** takes in many pieces of visual information at once, and that input first enters **sensory memory**. The store briefly preserves it so that **attention** can select what matters, and most of the rest is not kept indefinitely."]}, {"id": 12, "a": ["**Sensory registration** and **durable learning** are different outcomes. Information can register briefly in sensory memory and still never become durable, which is why seeing a whole directory does not mean you remember it."]}, {"id": 13, "a": ["**Attention** is the step that moves information forward. Focusing on one phone number transfers it into **short-term memory**, where it is available for immediate use. **Selection** limits which part of the large sensory input receives further processing, so the other entries do not have to stay accessible."]}, {"id": 14, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** means repeating the number to keep it available long enough to dial. It works on information already held in short-term memory and prolongs access that would otherwise be lost to loss or interference."]}, {"id": 15, "a": ["A **rehearsed number can become represented in long-term memory**. Once it is, later use no longer depends on the directory still being visible, because the number is held independently of its source. The active short-term representation is distinct from what is retained beyond the original encounter."]}, {"id": 16, "a": ["The number can later be **retrieved from long-term memory into short-term memory** for dialing. Remembering therefore requires bringing stored information back into an active form, so information flows between the stores in both directions, not only toward long-term storage."]}, {"id": 17, "a": ["An **icon** is a **fleeting visual representation**, and an **echo** is a **brief continuation of auditory information** after a sound. They differ in modality, and neither is a durable representation used to remember information much later."]}, {"id": 18, "a": ["**Persistence of vision** is the brief retention of the perception of light. It contributes to a sparkler’s trail and to the continuity experienced from film frames, which shows that sensory memory briefly holds information to fill the gaps between moments of input."]}, {"id": 19, "a": ["A large capacity does not mean a long duration. **Sensory memory** holds a lot, but the information **decays quickly**, so most of it is lost unless attention selects it for further processing."]}, {"id": 20, "a": ["**Sperling (1960)** flashed an array of letters and asked participants to report what they had seen. The question was whether the limited report reflected limited initial registration or rapid loss before every letter could be reported. Changing the report requirement made it possible to compare these two explanations."]}, {"id": 21, "a": ["Sperling used three report conditions. In **whole report**, participants report as many letters as they can. In **partial report**, a tone after the display identifies one row to recall. In **delayed partial report**, that tone comes after an extra delay, so the observer cannot prepare in advance."]}, {"id": 22, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), the tone comes after the display, so the row is not identified until after viewing. Participants could not know in advance which row to memorize, so accurate report of whichever row was cued shows that information about several rows was initially available."]}, {"id": 23, "a": ["Sperling’s array contained **X M L T, A F N B and C D Z P**. The cue selects a row after the display has gone. When the cue is delayed, the sensory trace fades, so the delay measures how quickly the trace is lost."]}, {"id": 24, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), the procedure separates **initial availability** from **the ability to report every item before it disappears**. Whole report mixes the two together, while the cue lets availability be measured without requiring everything to be reported."]}, {"id": 25, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), **whole report averaged 4.5 of 12 letters**, or 37.5 percent. **Immediate partial report averaged 3.3 of 4 letters**, about 82 percent, whichever row was cued. **Delayed partial report averaged 1 of 4**, or 25 percent. The large immediate cue advantage shows that more was available than whole report could recover."]}, {"id": 26, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), the whole-report limit of about four to five letters does not show that only that many were perceived. Because immediate partial report recovered accurately whichever row was cued, the report limit concealed greater initial availability."]}, {"id": 27, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), **sensory memory** has large capacity but rapid decay. Most information is lost within about one second unless it is selected for further processing. The large capacity is what lets any cued row be reported, and the rapid decay is what makes whole report fall short."]}, {"id": 28, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), as the cue is delayed, partial-report accuracy falls toward whole-report performance, even though the display is unchanged. The pattern links the immediate advantage to a **rapidly fading representation**, and it does not support keeping the whole visual array indefinitely."]}, {"id": 29, "a": ["**Short-term memory** holds small amounts briefly and is disrupted by interruption and interference. **Memory span** is the amount a person can recall accurately in a given task, so it depends on the task as well as on memory."]}, {"id": 30, "a": ["The **traditional estimate** of memory span is **seven plus or minus two units**. That figure depends on the kind of units and the task, so it should not be applied to every visual-memory capacity result."]}, {"id": 31, "a": ["**Recoding** reorganizes or modifies information while it is in short-term memory. It is a control process that changes the form of the information, which is different from simply repeating it."]}, {"id": 32, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** repeats information to prolong its availability. **Elaborative rehearsal** links new information with existing long-term knowledge, which supports longer-term learning. Repeating a label and explaining how it connects to what you already know are different control processes with different results."]}, {"id": 33, "a": ["**Short-term memory** holds selected sensory input and information retrieved from long-term memory. This is what lets you understand a sentence, do arithmetic, dial a number and navigate. It acts as a window on the present, and its active availability differs from the large body of knowledge stored outside current awareness."]}, {"id": 34, "a": ["**Clive Wearing** is a case of **memory impairment**. Different memory functions, such as keeping immediate information and remembering across events, need to be distinguished, and each has to be considered on its own."]}, {"id": 35, "a": ["**Duration** is how long information stays available, and **capacity** is how much can be held. They are separate properties, so a memory store can be described by both."]}, {"id": 36, "a": ["**Rehearsal can extend duration**, and **chunking can extend effective capacity**. A measured result can therefore reflect the strategy a person used as well as the memory system under study, so researchers must account for these control processes when measuring the stores."]}, {"id": 37, "a": ["In **Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, participants were shown three letters followed by a number. They then counted backward by threes during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds before recalling the letters."]}, {"id": 38, "a": ["Counting backward by threes prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal, so participants cannot simply keep repeating the letters. In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), comparing the delays therefore measures retention when rehearsal is blocked."]}, {"id": 39, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), recall on the **first trial** stays high at three seconds and remains relatively high at eighteen seconds. No earlier trigrams compete with the current one, which limits the claim that delay alone produces the full later decline."]}, {"id": 40, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), by the **third trial** recall at eighteen seconds is lower than on the first. Earlier trigrams now compete with the current one, so the same delay has different effects depending on what came before."]}, {"id": 41, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), after **many trials** recall at longer delays is substantially reduced. Accumulated earlier material interferes with the trigram being tested, so the growing difference across trial positions points to interference, not only to the passage of eighteen seconds."]}, {"id": 42, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the **average over all trials** shows a decline from shorter to longer delays. But the average combines early trials with little interference and later trials with more, so it can hide important differences by trial position. An explanation has to account for both the average and the changing pattern."]}, {"id": 43, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), recall falls with longer delays, but the effect depends on where a trial falls in the sequence. Delay and accumulated interference jointly affect retention, which challenges reading every loss as pure time-based decay."]}, {"id": 44, "a": ["**Decay** is loss associated with the passage of time. **Proactive interference** occurs when earlier information disrupts remembering newer information. The Brown–Peterson pattern includes evidence for interference because earlier trials affect later recall."]}, {"id": 45, "a": ["The effective duration of short-term memory is about **15 to 20 seconds**, but the outcome depends on rehearsal and interference conditions. The number is therefore not fixed regardless of what the person is doing."]}, {"id": 46, "a": ["**Interference** occurs when memories compete with or replace one another, so forgetting cannot be explained by elapsed time alone. Retroactive and proactive interference differ in the direction of that competition, and to label one correctly you must identify which information is being recalled."]}, {"id": 47, "a": ["**Retroactive interference** occurs when new information disrupts memory for older information. Learning a new phone number can make the old number harder to retrieve. The affected memory is the old one, and the interfering information arrived later."]}, {"id": 48, "a": ["**Proactive interference** occurs when old information disrupts memory for newer information. Previous parking locations can interfere with remembering where the car is parked today. Routine events are especially vulnerable because similar memories compete."]}, {"id": 49, "a": ["Both kinds of interference can be reduced when events are distinctive. Distinct events compete less with one another, which is why routine events with many similar memories are the most vulnerable."]}, {"id": 50, "a": ["**Digit span** measures the longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order. Capacity differs between individuals and can be shaped by strategies. Recalling the digits without their order does not satisfy an ordered-span task."]}, {"id": 51, "a": ["The span is the longest list reproduced completely and in order. Lists grow from four items to ten, and one successful short list does not set the upper limit. A single missed digit makes a longer list incorrect under this scoring rule."]}, {"id": 52, "a": ["In **Miller’s (1956)** paper, The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two, Miller proposed an influential capacity description. The units remembered can differ across tasks, so the title should not be taken as proof that every short-term system stores exactly seven raw sensory elements."]}, {"id": 53, "a": ["Miller’s estimate applies to units such as **digits, words, numbers or phrases**, and a unit can contain different amounts of information. The estimate therefore counts units, not equal pieces of raw sensory information."]}, {"id": 54, "a": ["**Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** compared colored squares with more complex objects using change detection. Their emphasis is on the amount or complexity of information in each item, rather than on a fixed number of equally sized items."]}, {"id": 55, "a": ["**Visual short-term memory (VSTM)** is measured by comparing displays after a delay. Its capacity can be smaller than span for digit lists or unrelated words, and researchers still debate how many modality- or domain-specific stores are needed. Different tasks do not have to measure one identical capacity."]}, {"id": 56, "a": ["In change detection, participants view an initial array, **S1**, briefly, here for 100 milliseconds. Once it disappears, its colors and shapes are no longer directly available, so the relevant visual properties must be retained and compared later with memory."]}, {"id": 57, "a": ["The short-term representation must preserve the **features needed for comparison**. Color, shape, orientation and complexity can all affect how demanding that retention is."]}, {"id": 58, "a": ["The second display, **S2**, appears for 2,000 milliseconds, and participants judge whether it is the same as or different from S1. Correct performance requires comparing S2 with the retained S1, so accuracy depends on memory for the first display."]}, {"id": 59, "a": ["Accuracy falls sharply when set size exceeds about four items, which gives a **capacity of about three to four items in visual working memory**. This differs from the seven-plus-or-minus-two estimate for other tasks, and the comparison depends on stimulus type, delay and the response required."]}, {"id": 60, "a": ["Same–different performance depends on **stimulus complexity**. Colored squares support a higher capacity estimate than more complex polygons or shaded cubes, because treating every stimulus as one equal item hides how much information each one requires."]}, {"id": 61, "a": ["Familiarity and the structure attributed to a stimulus can change what counts as a unit of information. Chinese-character-like forms were also compared, and a capacity for one stimulus class is not a universal estimate for all visual objects."]}, {"id": 62, "a": ["**Chunking** combines smaller units into larger meaningful ones. **Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** trained a student to reach a **digit span of 79** by using familiar running times to organize the digits. The gain depends on knowledge-based organization, not on expanding every raw memory limit equally."]}, {"id": 63, "a": ["**Chase and Simon (1973)** found that chess expertise supports grouping meaningful positions. The advantage again comes from knowledge-based organization, since the expert recognizes familiar relationships as single units rather than from a larger raw memory limit."]}, {"id": 64, "a": ["In **Chase and Simon (1973)**, chess masters recalled more correct piece placements than beginners for meaningful game arrangements, but the advantage is greatly reduced for random arrangements. Expertise supports chunks of familiar relationships, and randomizing those relationships removes most of the benefit even though the same kinds of pieces remain."]}, {"id": 65, "a": ["**Control processes** include rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable and directing attention. The **memory palace** is an example of organizing information through a remembered spatial structure. These active operations lead toward **working memory**, which concerns using and managing currently available information."]}, {"id": 66, "a": ["Attention selects part of the large sensory input for short-term memory, as when focusing on a phone number moves it forward. Rehearsal then keeps that information available and can support its transfer to long-term memory, while retrieval brings stored information back into short-term memory. The stores are connected by control processes running in both directions."]}, {"id": 67, "a": ["Sensory memory has **large capacity but decays quickly**, with most information lost within about one second unless selected. Short-term memory holds only **five to seven items for about 15 to 20 seconds**. The contrast is large capacity with very short duration against small capacity with somewhat longer duration."]}, {"id": 68, "a": ["In Sperling (1960), whole report averaged only 4.5 of 12 letters, yet immediate partial report reached about 82 percent for whichever row was cued. Because the cue came after the display, far more than four or five letters must have been available at first, so the limit came from rapid decay before everything could be reported."]}, {"id": 69, "a": ["Sperling’s (1960) delayed cue and the Brown–Peterson counting delay both vary the time before report and test what happens as time passes. In Sperling’s task, delaying the cue lets the sensory trace fade. In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), counting fills the delay so rehearsal is blocked. One tests a sensory trace and the other tests retention without rehearsal."]}, {"id": 70, "a": ["**Decay** is loss with the passage of time, and **interference** is memories competing with or replacing one another. The Brown–Peterson pattern shows that forgetting is not explained by elapsed time alone, because earlier trials affect later recall. Delay and accumulated interference jointly affect retention."]}, {"id": 71, "a": ["The Brown–Peterson pattern shows **proactive interference**, because earlier trigrams disrupt recall of the newer one. **Retroactive interference** runs the other way, with new information disrupting older memories, as when a new phone number makes an old one harder to retrieve. To label it correctly, identify which information is being recalled and whether the competitor came earlier or later."]}, {"id": 72, "a": ["**Maintenance rehearsal** prolongs availability in short-term memory by repeating information. **Elaborative rehearsal** links new information to existing long-term knowledge, which supports longer-term learning. One keeps information available for now, and the other helps it last."]}, {"id": 73, "a": ["**Duration** is how long information stays available, and **capacity** is how much can be held. Rehearsal can extend duration and chunking can extend effective capacity, so a measured result can reflect the strategy as well as the memory system being studied, and control processes must be accounted for."]}, {"id": 74, "a": ["There are several estimates. The modal model describes five to seven items, the traditional estimate is seven plus or minus two units, and visual short-term memory is about three to four items. Each depends on the kind of units and the task, and stimulus complexity also changes the estimate, so there is no single capacity number."]}, {"id": 75, "a": ["**Digit span** measures the longest sequence of digits recalled correctly in order, so it depends on ordered recall of a growing list. **Change detection** compares a second display with a first display retained after a delay and asks only whether they are the same or different. The tasks use different stimuli and responses, which is why their estimates differ."]}, {"id": 76, "a": ["**Miller (1956)** describes capacity in **units** such as digits, words, numbers or phrases, and the units can differ across tasks. **Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** emphasize the amount or complexity of information in each item. So the first counts units, and the second says that a unit can carry more or less information."]}, {"id": 77, "a": ["In **Chase and Simon (1973)**, chess masters recalled more correct placements for meaningful game arrangements because they could group pieces into chunks of familiar relationships. Random arrangements remove those relationships, so the advantage is greatly reduced. The pieces are the same, so the difference comes from knowledge-based organization."]}, {"id": 78, "a": ["**Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** trained a student to reach a digit span of 79 by using familiar running times to organize sequences, and **Chase and Simon (1973)** found that chess expertise supports grouping meaningful positions. In both, knowledge-based organization raises what can be remembered, rather than expanding every raw memory limit equally."]}, {"id": 79, "a": ["**Encoding, storage and retrieval** are operations on information, while **sensory, short-term and long-term memory** are the stores. A phone number is encoded into a usable form, retained in a store, and retrieved from long-term memory back into short-term memory for dialing."]}, {"id": 80, "a": ["The **memory tree** and the **modal model** both treat sensory, short-term and long-term memory as distinct. The tree shows the kinds of long-term memory, with declarative and procedural memory inside it, while the modal model adds rehearsal as a control process and shows how information moves between stores."]}, {"id": 81, "a": ["An **icon** is a fleeting visual representation held in sensory memory. **Persistence of vision** is the brief retention of light perception that contributes to a sparkler’s trail and to the continuity of film frames. Both involve very brief retention, and neither is a durable memory of the kind used to remember something much later."]}, {"id": 82, "a": ["Different memory functions, such as maintaining immediate information and remembering across events, need to be distinguished. A case like **Clive Wearing’s** shows why, since a case name alone does not specify every affected memory system or every preserved ability. Analyzing the case function by function avoids assuming that one memory system explains everything."]}, {"id": 83, "a": ["Short-term memory includes selected sensory input and information retrieved from long-term memory, so current thought draws on both. Dialing a number depends on retrieval from long-term memory into short-term memory, which is why the window on the present relies on stored knowledge as well as new input."]}, {"id": 84, "a": ["Control processes such as rehearsal, strategies for making material memorable and directing attention are active operations on available information. **Working memory** concerns using and managing that currently available information, so thinking about these operations leads directly toward it."]}, {"id": 85, "a": ["In **Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, counting backward by threes prevents ordinary maintenance rehearsal, so retention is measured without simple repetition. Chunking extends effective capacity, so a result can reflect the strategy as well as the store, and researchers must account for control processes such as rehearsal and chunking."]}, {"id": 86, "a": ["In **Sperling (1960)**, the whole-report limit hid the larger initial availability that only the cued partial report revealed. In change detection (**Alvarez and Cavanagh, 2004**), the capacity estimate depends on stimulus type, delay and the response required. In both cases, the way capacity is measured shapes what appears to be stored."]}, {"id": 87, "a": ["The modal model treats short-term memory as **passive storage**, and that view is too simple. Keeping information available also involves **control processes**, which are operations that act on the information. A memory model has to account for both holding something in mind and doing something with it."]}, {"id": 88, "a": ["**Baddeley and Hitch (1974)** proposed **working memory** as a limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information. Storage keeps information available for immediate use, and manipulation works with that information while it remains available."]}, {"id": 89, "a": ["Comprehension, learning and reasoning need both functions at once. Comprehending material means keeping information available while relating its parts, and reasoning means holding the information used in a decision while carrying out the operations that lead to an answer."]}, {"id": 90, "a": ["In Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974) definition, **manipulation** changes or works with information while it remains available, and **temporary storage** keeps it available for immediate use. Working memory covers both, so it is not just a holding place."]}, {"id": 91, "a": ["Multiplying **43 times 6 in your head** needs storage and processing together. First, 3 × 6 = 18: the **8 is held** as the ones digit, which is storage, and the **1 is carried** over the 4, which is an operation. Next, 6 × 4 = 24, plus the carried 1 gives 25, and combining that with the held 8 gives 258."]}, {"id": 92, "a": ["Working memory accounts for keeping an intermediate result available while other parts of the problem change. A system that only held information would describe the retained 8, but the calculation also requires coordinating which number is stored, which is carried and which operation comes next."]}, {"id": 93, "a": ["In **Atkinson and Shiffrin’s (1968)** model a single short-term component holds different types of information for a short time, and the emphasis is on temporary availability. In **Baddeley and Hitch’s (1974)** model, the system is separated into the **central executive**, the **visuospatial sketchpad** and the **phonological loop**, so visual and spatial information can be held apart from verbal information, with the central executive coordinating them."]}, {"id": 94, "a": ["The **episodic buffer** is a fourth component added by Baddeley in 2000. It belongs to the expanded model, not to the original three-component proposal, so the model now distinguishes specialized temporary stores, executive control and a component that integrates information."]}, {"id": 95, "a": ["The **phonological loop** maintains verbal and auditory information. Its **phonological store** holds that information, and its **articulatory rehearsal process** keeps it active. Repeating verbal material refreshes information that would otherwise decay in the store."]}, {"id": 96, "a": ["**Articulatory** refers to producing speech. Rehearsal can involve repeating the sounds internally rather than aloud, and this rehearsal process acts on the store to keep the verbal information available."]}, {"id": 97, "a": ["The **visuospatial sketchpad** holds visual and spatial information. **Visual** information concerns the appearance of something, and **spatial** information concerns where its parts are and how they are arranged. These are different demands from maintaining the sounds of words."]}, {"id": 98, "a": ["The **central executive** pulls information from long-term memory, coordinates the other components, and directs and maintains attention. It controls how the temporary stores contribute to the current task, and it is not a store for a particular kind of information."]}, {"id": 99, "a": ["The working-memory components are interacting parts, not one undifferentiated short-term store. Sensory memory feeds incoming information in, and working memory operates on what is available for the task. The connections between components let a task use more than one kind of representation, and long-term knowledge can be made available during the task under the central executive’s coordination."]}, {"id": 100, "a": ["A calculation can use **verbal rehearsal of a result together with a visual representation of the digits**. The phonological loop supports the verbal part and the visuospatial sketchpad supports the visual part, and the connections between components are what allow the two to work together."]}, {"id": 101, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect** is confusion between letters or words that sound alike, even when their written forms differ. Because the confusion follows sound, the items are being kept as sound patterns."]}, {"id": 102, "a": ["The **word-length effect** is that memory for lists is better for short words than for long words. Longer words take more time to rehearse and to produce during recall, so maintaining a list depends partly on how quickly its contents can be repeated."]}, {"id": 103, "a": ["**Articulatory suppression** is speaking while trying to remember other material. The speaking occupies the rehearsal process, so it interferes with the repetition that normally keeps verbal items available, and memory is reduced because the competing speech uses the process that rehearsal needs."]}, {"id": 104, "a": ["The three effects concern different aspects of verbal maintenance. **Similarity** is about confusion between sound patterns, **word length** is about the time needed to repeat items, and **suppression** is about interference with the rehearsal process itself. Each depends on how the phonological loop keeps verbal information available."]}, {"id": 105, "a": ["**Conrad (1964)** briefly flashed target letters for participants to see and then asked them to write the letters down. In half of the presentations the letters were similar, and in half they were not. The task required keeping letter identities after a brief visual presentation."]}, {"id": 106, "a": ["In Conrad (1964), errors were **more likely to substitute a letter that sounded like the target** than one that merely looked like it. An E was likely to be replaced by a B, whose name sounds similar, while a visually similar substitution such as F for E was less likely."]}, {"id": 107, "a": ["In Conrad (1964) the letters entered through vision, but the confusions followed their spoken names. So a seen letter can be converted into a sound-based representation, and the phonological loop contributes to that conversion as well as to holding the result. The E–B confusion shows this, because the overlap is in pronunciation and not in the visible shapes."]}, {"id": 108, "a": ["**Presentation format** is what participants see, and the **memory code** is the form in which the information is maintained. In Conrad (1964) the letters were presented visually, but the sound-based substitutions show that they were maintained as sounds, so letters can be confused by pronunciation rather than appearance."]}, {"id": 109, "a": ["**Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)** found that people remembered more short words than long words. The word-length effect concerns how long the items take to pronounce and rehearse, so a list of longer words takes more time to repeat and fewer words can be refreshed in the same brief period."]}, {"id": 110, "a": ["**Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found that Welsh–English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English. **Digit span** is the number of digits that can be retained and reported in order. The children knew both languages, but Welsh number names take longer to pronounce, so the difference follows pronunciation time and not the numbers themselves."]}, {"id": 111, "a": ["Span is related to the amount of verbal material that can be said in about 1.5 to 2 seconds. Faster rehearsal allows more items to be refreshed within that interval, and longer pronunciation time reduces the number of separate items that can be kept active together."]}, {"id": 112, "a": ["When a person speaks while memorizing, the competing speech keeps the phonological loop busy. The person has less opportunity to rehearse the items that must be remembered, so recall becomes worse. The interference is consistent with a system that uses speech-related rehearsal to maintain verbal information."]}, {"id": 113, "a": ["Under articulatory suppression the **word-length effect is reduced**. Short words normally have an advantage because they can be rehearsed more quickly than long words, but when rehearsal is occupied by another speaking task, neither list receives ordinary rehearsal, so that advantage shrinks."]}, {"id": 114, "a": ["Under articulatory suppression the **phonological similarity effect is also reduced**. Verbal rehearsal and the conversion of seen items into sounds are disrupted, so the sound-based overlap between the remembered items has less influence on performance."]}, {"id": 115, "a": ["The **visual** information held by the sketchpad includes the appearance and shape of something. The **spatial** information includes the arrangement and relative locations of its parts, which allows that arrangement to remain available without continuous visual input. Both are held by the visuospatial sketchpad."]}, {"id": 116, "a": ["The **visuospatial sketchpad supports mental imagery**. A visual representation can be kept in mind and used during a task even when the object is no longer being viewed. Holding a shape in mind is a different demand from holding the sounds of a sentence, and both components work with the central executive and long-term knowledge."]}, {"id": 117, "a": ["In **Brooks (1968)**, participants memorized a sentence and decided, for each word, whether it was a noun. The words had to remain available while participants classified them one by one. Some answered by speaking and others by pointing."]}, {"id": 118, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), **pointing was easier than speaking**. Holding the sentence occupied the phonological loop, and speaking the classifications also required verbal processing, so the two competed. Pointing used a different response format while the sketchpad was available, so the verbal memory demand was separated from the response demand."]}, {"id": 119, "a": ["In **Brooks (1968)**, participants memorized the shape of an F, mentally followed its outline, and classified each corner as an inside corner or an outside corner. An inside corner makes an indentation in the shape, and an outside corner projects around its outer boundary. They either spoke their answers or pointed to the OUT and IN response locations."]}, {"id": 120, "a": ["In Brooks’s (1968) F task, **speaking was easier than pointing**. Holding and following the F used the visuospatial sketchpad, and pointing to spatially arranged responses added a competing spatial demand. The phonological loop was available to support spoken responses, so speaking competed less with the shape being held."]}, {"id": 121, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), tasks are easier when the information held and the operation performed use **different types of short-term memory**. For a remembered sentence speaking competes, so pointing is easier. For a remembered shape pointing competes, so speaking is easier. These opposite advantages support some independence between verbal and visual–spatial memory."]}, {"id": 122, "a": ["**Shepard and Metzler (1971)** presented pairs of block-built objects at different orientations. Participants judged whether the objects matched despite the difference in orientation, which requires comparing the arrangement of parts and not just noticing that one picture is tilted."]}, {"id": 123, "a": ["**Mental rotation** is turning a representation in mind to compare how its parts line up. **Picture-plane rotation** changes orientation within the flat viewing plane, and **depth rotation** changes it in three-dimensional space. In either case, the task requires comparing the arrangement of parts."]}, {"id": 124, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, for picture-plane pairs, reaction time increased with the angle of rotation, approximately linearly. A larger angle requires a larger mental transformation before the objects can be aligned, which points to an active operation on a spatial representation rather than storage of an unchanged picture."]}, {"id": 125, "a": ["The **central executive** does the work of working memory by directing and maintaining attention. It focuses on relevant information, divides attention across demands and switches attention when the task changes. It also suppresses irrelevant information, coordinates the sketchpad and the phonological loop, and performs calculations."]}, {"id": 126, "a": ["**Miyake and colleagues (2000)** distinguish **updating**, which changes the information currently maintained, **shifting**, which changes the task or operation being performed, and **inhibition**, which suppresses information or responses that interfere with the current goal."]}, {"id": 127, "a": ["The central executive is sometimes criticized as a **homunculus**, a little internal agent assigned the job of controlling everything else. Naming a controller does not explain how control is performed. Separating updating, shifting and inhibition specifies different operations inside what would otherwise be one broad label."]}, {"id": 128, "a": ["**Focusing**, **dividing** and **switching** attention are operations that control processing. Focusing selects relevant information, dividing spreads attention across demands, and switching changes attention when the task changes. They are part of how the central executive directs and maintains attention."]}, {"id": 129, "a": ["**Baddeley (2000)** added the **episodic buffer** as a fourth component. It has a limited capacity of about four chunks, and a chunk is a unit that combines related information. The capacity therefore concerns organized units rather than necessarily four individual words."]}, {"id": 130, "a": ["The episodic buffer binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad and long-term memory into **integrated episodes**. Binding means keeping different kinds of information together as one connected representation, so verbal, visual or spatial information and existing knowledge can contribute to a single remembered episode."]}, {"id": 131, "a": ["People can immediately recall about **16 words** from a meaningful sentence, which is well beyond their span for unrelated words. Meaning and long-term language knowledge let the words be kept as connected material. Some amnesic patients can also immediately recall a just-heard story, so organized episodes can be held even when longer-term memory is impaired."]}, {"id": 132, "a": ["**Vogel and colleagues (2005)** first measured participants’ working-memory capacity and sorted them into higher- and lower-capacity groups. They then recorded **event-related potentials (ERPs)** during a visual memory task. An ERP is an electrical brain response measured in relation to a task event."]}, {"id": 133, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), a cue directed attention to one side of a display. Red bars were shown briefly, with a retention interval of 900 milliseconds in which the bars were absent, and then a test display asked whether a relevant orientation had changed. The memory display lasted 100 milliseconds."]}, {"id": 134, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), the more complex displays included **irrelevant blue bars** alongside the relevant red bars. Ignoring the blue bars required selection within the display, so comparing target-only and distractor conditions tested how irrelevant items entered working-memory processing."]}, {"id": 135, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005) the memory-related response continues into the retention interval, after the display has disappeared. It has a **negative polarity**, so a larger response means a stronger negative deflection rather than a more positive voltage."]}, {"id": 136, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), for **high-capacity participants** the response is greater with four relevant items than with two. Adding two irrelevant blue items to two relevant items changes little, so those participants kept the distractors out and their response stayed close to the two-target condition."]}, {"id": 137, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), for **low-capacity participants** adding the irrelevant items raises the response toward the four-item condition. Their working-memory processing takes in more of the irrelevant material, so effective capacity depends on which items enter memory as well as on how many targets there are."]}, {"id": 138, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), **working-memory capacity is positively correlated with filtering efficiency**. Participants with higher capacity tend to have higher filtering-efficiency values, meaning they exclude irrelevant items from working-memory processing more effectively."]}, {"id": 139, "a": ["In Vogel and colleagues (2005), people with higher measured capacity are less distracted by irrelevant items. A lower memory score can reflect competing material occupying processing that targets needed, so individual differences in working memory include differences in selection and control, not only in how much relevant information is retained."]}, {"id": 140, "a": ["The modal model’s short-term memory is a single component for temporary availability, while working memory has separate components for verbal, visual–spatial and integrated information, plus executive control. Working memory also includes manipulation, so it is both a store and a system for operating on what is held."]}, {"id": 141, "a": ["In 43 × 6, the retained 8 is storage, but carrying the 1 and performing the next multiplication and addition are operations. The system has to hold and manipulate information together, which a single passive store cannot do."]}, {"id": 142, "a": ["The phonological loop maintains verbal and auditory information, and the visuospatial sketchpad maintains visual and spatial information. The central executive is not a store. It coordinates both, directs attention and draws on long-term memory, so the loop and the sketchpad differ in what they hold, while the executive differs in what it does."]}, {"id": 143, "a": ["Similarity, word length and suppression are different aspects of verbal maintenance. Suppression occupies rehearsal, and it also reduces the word-length and similarity effects, which is what ties the three together."]}, {"id": 144, "a": ["In Conrad (1964), the letters were seen, but the confusions followed sound, such as E replaced by B. The maintained code was therefore phonological and not visual, and presentation format, what was seen, is different from the memory code, how it was kept."]}, {"id": 145, "a": ["Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) found that more short words than long words were remembered, and Ellis and Hennelly (1980) found a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English. Both show that span depends on how long the items take to say, since longer words and Welsh number names take longer to rehearse, so fewer items fit into about 1.5 to 2 seconds."]}, {"id": 146, "a": ["The phonological store holds verbal and auditory information, the visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information, and the episodic buffer binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Each component holds a different kind of information, and the buffer is the one that combines them."]}, {"id": 147, "a": ["In Brooks (1968), the sentence task was easier with pointing and the F task was easier with speaking. In each case the response was easier when it used a different type of memory from the one holding the information, which shows that verbal and visual–spatial memory are partly independent."]}, {"id": 148, "a": ["Brooks (1968) varied whether the response used speaking or pointing, and found the response was easier when it used a different type of memory. Vogel and colleagues (2005) added irrelevant blue bars and found that higher-capacity participants kept them out. In both, a competing or irrelevant demand shows how working memory handles interference."]}, {"id": 149, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, the linear increase in reaction time with angle suggests an active transformation of a spatial representation. That matches working memory as manipulation, not only storage."]}, {"id": 150, "a": ["The central executive directs attention, suppresses irrelevant information and coordinates the components, while the stores hold verbal or visual–spatial contents. Updating, shifting and inhibition describe operations of control rather than contents that are held."]}, {"id": 151, "a": ["Naming a controller does not explain how control is performed, which is the homunculus objection. Separating updating, shifting and inhibition, as **Miyake and colleagues (2000)** distinguish them, specifies different operations within what would otherwise be one broad executive label."]}, {"id": 152, "a": ["People recall about 16 words of a meaningful sentence, beyond their span for unrelated words, and amnesic patients can immediately recall a story. These findings suggest an integrated temporary store that draws on long-term knowledge, which the loop and the sketchpad alone do not provide. That is the role of the episodic buffer."]}, {"id": 153, "a": ["The buffer’s capacity is about four chunks, which are organized units and not necessarily four individual words. Meaningful material can therefore exceed the span for unrelated words, which fits the finding that about 16 words of a sentence can be recalled."]}, {"id": 154, "a": ["Differences in capacity include selection and control. The central executive suppresses irrelevant information and directs attention, and in **Vogel and colleagues (2005)** higher-capacity participants kept distractors out, which is a matter of selection."]}, {"id": 155, "a": ["In **Vogel and colleagues (2005)**, high-capacity participants kept the blue distractors out, while low-capacity participants let them in. Effective capacity therefore depends on selection, meaning which items get in, and not only on how many items are shown."]}, {"id": 156, "a": ["**Conrad (1964)** shows phonological loop coding, because letters that were seen were maintained as sounds and confused by their spoken names. **Brooks (1968)** shows competition within a component: speaking competes with the loop in the sentence task, and pointing competes with the sketchpad in the F task."]}, {"id": 157, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, reaction time rose with the angle of rotation, which fits an operation on a spatial representation. In **Brooks (1968)**, holding the shape of an F used the sketchpad, so pointing to spatially arranged responses competed with it. Both show the sketchpad holding and working on visual and spatial information."]}, {"id": 158, "a": ["A single short-term store keeps information temporarily available. Working memory adds operations performed on held information, as in calculation, reasoning and comprehension, through separate components and executive control."]}, {"id": 159, "a": ["**Conrad (1964)** found that seen letters were confused by their spoken names. **Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found that digit span was shorter in Welsh because the number names take longer to pronounce. Both show that verbal memory depends on speech sounds, one through confusions and the other through pronunciation time."]}, {"id": 160, "a": ["A **problem** is a situation with an **obstacle between a present state and a goal**, and the solution is not immediately obvious. The obstacle is what prevents an immediate move from where you are to where you want to be, which is why a problem is more than a goal that has been stated."]}, {"id": 161, "a": ["The **present state** is the situation as it currently exists, and the **goal** is the situation the person is trying to reach. The **obstacle** prevents a direct move from one to the other, so the problem lies in finding the route between them."]}, {"id": 162, "a": ["Knowing the desired arrangement does not supply the sequence that produces it. In the numbered-tile puzzle a person can say exactly what a solution looks like and still have a problem, because the route from the starting arrangement is unclear. Reaching the goal requires changing the positions of the tiles, not just recognizing the numbers."]}, {"id": 163, "a": ["**Gestalt psychologists** took a perceptual approach to learning, problem solving, and attitudes and beliefs as well as to perception. **Koffka (1935)** is associated with this broader approach. The central idea is that **problem solving involves representing a problem in the mind**, so the organization a person gives the problem affects which solution becomes available."]}, {"id": 164, "a": ["**Restructuring** means reorganizing the problem’s representation. The same physical arrangement can be represented in different ways, and a relationship that is not obvious from the first encounter can become usable once the representation changes."]}, {"id": 165, "a": ["In **Köhler’s circle problem** you must find the length of line x when the circle’s radius is r. The black line is one diagonal of a rectangle whose lower-right corner is at the circle’s center, and the dashed line from the upper-left corner on the circle to the center is a radius. The two diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so x = r. The key change is recognizing the rectangle rather than treating the line as an isolated unknown."]}, {"id": 166, "a": ["**Insight** is the sudden realization of a problem’s solution. A person first perceives an object or problem one way and then represents it differently, and discovering a crucial element in the new representation makes the solution available all at once."]}, {"id": 167, "a": ["Gestalt psychologists connected reorganization with the **Aha experience**. Rather than a series of increasingly complete steps, the solver experiences a sudden change from not having the solution to recognizing it."]}, {"id": 168, "a": ["No. Some problems permit a **more methodical process**, in which successive operations bring the person closer to an answer. Sudden realization describes insight, but it does not describe every way that a problem can be solved."]}, {"id": 169, "a": ["**Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** compared **insight problems**, including the triangle and chain problems, with **noninsight problems**, including algebra. Participants attempted the problems and repeatedly judged how close they felt to a solution, so the study concerned the course of solving and not only whether the final answer was right."]}, {"id": 170, "a": ["For insight problems, Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) predicted that people would not be very good at anticipating how near they were to a solution, because a crucial change in representation can produce the answer without a steadily rising feeling of progress. For noninsight problems, successive algebraic operations give information about progress, so perceived closeness should rise as the solution approaches."]}, {"id": 171, "a": ["In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), participants made **warmth judgments every 15 seconds**. Hot ratings meant feeling close to the solution and colder ratings meant feeling farther away. These reports gave a repeated measure of subjective progress that could be compared with when the participant actually solved the problem."]}, {"id": 172, "a": ["The **triangle problem** begins with ten blue dots arranged in four rows so the triangle points upward. The task is to move exactly three dots so that it points toward the bottom of the page. The original top dot becomes a new bottom point beneath the center of the original bottom row, and the two outer dots of the bottom row move to the outer ends of the two-dot row. That gives a downward triangle with rows of four, three, two and one."]}, {"id": 173, "a": ["In the **chain problem**, four short sections of chain must become one closed loop. Opening a link costs 2 cents and closing it costs 3, and the budget is 15 cents, so the budget allows three opening-and-closing operations. Opening one three-link section into three separate links lets those links join the other three sections into a closed loop, using the full budget."]}, {"id": 174, "a": ["The task is to move exactly three dots, so seven must stay in place. Relocating the whole arrangement would break the task, so the solution has to come from moving a few specific dots, which is part of why a change of representation is needed."]}, {"id": 175, "a": ["In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), ratings for **algebra** increased gradually as the solution approached, so the feeling of getting closer developed over time. For **insight problems**, warmth stayed relatively low until close to the solution and then increased sharply, from about 3 to 7."]}, {"id": 176, "a": ["In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), a low rating shortly before an insight solution means the person did not yet feel close even though the answer was imminent. This contrast connects sudden insight with a different experience of progress from methodical solving. It does not mean that all successful problem solving is equally sudden."]}, {"id": 177, "a": ["**Fixation** is a tendency to focus on a specific characteristic of a problem in a way that prevents a solution, and Gestalt psychologists treated it as a major obstacle. A familiar interpretation can hold attention even when another interpretation would make the available materials useful."]}, {"id": 178, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** restricts an object to its familiar functions. The obstacle comes from the familiar use assigned to the object, not from a lack of useful material, so the object’s usual job blocks seeing another job it could do."]}, {"id": 179, "a": ["In the **candle problem**, a person has a candle, matches and their box, and tacks, and must mount the candle on a vertical corkboard so that it burns without dripping wax onto the floor. The matchbox can be tacked to the corkboard and used as a platform for the candle. Treating the box only as a container for matches blocks recognizing that support function."]}, {"id": 180, "a": ["**Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the candle problem**. Separating the box from its familiar contents made it easier to treat the box as an object with another possible use, so the presentation changed whether the support function became available."]}, {"id": 181, "a": ["In the **two-string problem**, two strings hang from the ceiling far enough apart that a person holding one cannot reach the other. A pair of pliers can be tied to one string and used as a weight. Setting that string swinging creates a pendulum, and the person can hold the other string and catch the weighted one when it swings close enough."]}, {"id": 182, "a": ["In the two-string problem, **functional fixedness** occurs because the familiar grip function of the pliers prevents their use as a weight. The useful property is their weight rather than their ability to grip, and reorganizing the object’s role makes the string move in a way that overcomes the reach problem."]}, {"id": 183, "a": ["When an experimenter accidentally set a string in motion, **most participants were able to solve the problem**. The moving string made its potential use as a pendulum available, so the hanging string changed from something that had to be reached where it hung into something that could be made to swing within reach."]}, {"id": 184, "a": ["A **mental set** is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones. It concerns the method of solving, so a person can keep applying a familiar procedure even when the problem allows another approach."]}, {"id": 185, "a": ["The **water-jug problem** asks for a required amount of water using three empty jugs with given capacities. Each problem specifies capacities for jugs A, B and C and a desired quantity, and the solver has to find a way to measure the target amount."]}, {"id": 186, "a": ["In the first water-jug problem, A holds 21 quarts, B holds 127, C holds 3, and the goal is 100. Filling B and removing one A-sized amount leaves 106, removing one C-sized amount leaves 103, and removing a second C-sized amount leaves 100. The procedure is B − A − 2C, and repeated success with it makes that procedure the expected method."]}, {"id": 187, "a": ["In problem 7 (A 15, B 39, C 3, goal 18) the familiar method works, since 39 − 15 − 6 gives 18, but A + C gives 18 more directly. In problem 8 (A 28, B 59, C 3, goal 25), A − C gives 25 directly, although B − A − 2C also works. A mental set can inhibit a simpler solution even when the familiar one remains possible."]}, {"id": 188, "a": ["**Newell and Simon (1956)** developed the Logic Theorist and described problem solving as a process involving **search**. Solving is the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution, in which the person looks for a way to reach the goal while working around obstacles."]}, {"id": 189, "a": ["A **problem space** consists of an **initial state**, the **intermediate states** reached along the way, and a **goal state** that meets the conditions for a solution. Knowing the initial and goal states does not say which intermediate states connect them, so solving requires finding a sequence that gets from one to the other."]}, {"id": 190, "a": ["**Operators** are the rules that specify which moves are allowed and which are not. Applying an operator changes the problem from one state to another, so the rules decide which transitions can belong to a possible solution, even when another movement is physically possible."]}, {"id": 191, "a": ["In the **Tower of Hanoi**, the disks start stacked on one peg and must be moved to the goal peg. A move transfers one disk at a time, and a disk can move only when no other disk is on top of it. A larger disk can never be placed on a smaller one, and this rule also applies to intermediate arrangements."]}, {"id": 192, "a": ["The starting stack is the **initial state**, the completed stack on the target peg is the **goal state**, and temporary distributions of disks across the pegs are the **intermediate states**. A useful route is a sequence of legal transitions through those intermediate states."]}, {"id": 193, "a": ["Smaller disks have to be moved out of the way before a larger disk can move, and they can occupy temporary positions on the other pegs while still obeying the size rule. Once the largest disk reaches the goal peg, the smaller disks must be rebuilt on it in the required order, so transferring the whole stack includes repeated transfers of smaller stacks."]}, {"id": 194, "a": ["The 64-disk version takes roughly a trillion years even if every correct move took one second. Adding disks multiplies the number of moves, and executing each move quickly does not remove the many intermediate rearrangements that the rules require."]}, {"id": 195, "a": ["The numbered-tile puzzle has eight tiles and one empty location on a three-by-three board. In the initial arrangement the rows are 8–empty–6, 5–4–7 and 2–3–1. In the goal arrangement they are empty–1–2, 3–4–5 and 6–7–8."]}, {"id": 196, "a": ["A tile being on the board does not mean its required position has been reached. The initial state is the complete starting arrangement, the goal is the desired arrangement of the same tiles and the empty space, and the current state must be connected to the goal through an allowable sequence of moves."]}, {"id": 197, "a": ["A **search tree** represents the possible states of the tile puzzle and the moves connecting them. Each state can allow several successors, and the solver chooses which branches to pursue, while a connected sequence of states gives a route to the goal."]}, {"id": 198, "a": ["From the start 2–8–3, 1–6–4, 7–empty–5, the empty space can shift left, up or right by exchanging places with an adjacent tile. Each result is a different intermediate state, even though every state contains the same eight tiles, and one state can allow several different successors."]}, {"id": 199, "a": ["The numbered labels identify positions in the search, and the small boards specify the actual tile arrangements. The distinction between a state and a move matters, because one state can permit several different successors under the same operator rules."]}, {"id": 200, "a": ["**Means–end analysis** organizes the search around reducing the difference between the current situation and the goal. The solver considers which changes would help remove an obstacle to the desired result, which Newell and Simon’s account of solving as search makes explicit."]}, {"id": 201, "a": ["A **subgoal** is a condition that makes part of the larger task possible, not the complete final answer. Achieving it removes an obstacle that blocks a required move, which lets the search move toward the overall goal."]}, {"id": 202, "a": ["The first subgoal is freeing the large disk, which requires moving the smallest disk to the third peg and the next disk to the middle peg. The second is freeing the third peg, by moving the smallest disk from the third peg onto the smaller stack on the middle peg. The third is moving the large disk to the third peg, which the size rule now allows, and the smaller disks still have to reach it."]}, {"id": 203, "a": ["The subgoals address different obstacles to the same move. One removes the material above the disk that must move, and another removes an obstruction at its destination. Means–end analysis can therefore require several coordinated changes before an action toward the overall goal becomes legal."]}, {"id": 204, "a": ["How a problem is stated can change its difficulty. The wording and presentation influence which relationships become part of the person’s representation, so two presentations of the same task can make a useful property easier or harder to recognize."]}, {"id": 205, "a": ["The **mutilated-checkerboard problem** asks whether a board with some squares removed can be covered with dominoes under the domino constraints, so the solver must consider whether a complete covering is possible at all."]}, {"id": 206, "a": ["In **Newell and Simon’s** approach, possible pathways from an initial state to a goal state can be specified. Identifying the states and the possible transitions makes the search explicit, and a solution requires an allowable route between the beginning and the required endpoint."]}, {"id": 207, "a": ["An ordinary checkerboard has 64 squares and can be covered by 32 dominoes. Removing two opposite corners leaves 62 squares, which matches the area of 31 dominoes, but the two removed corners are the same color. Every domino covers one square of each color, so the covering would need equal numbers of the two colors, and the board cannot be covered by 31 dominoes."]}, {"id": 208, "a": ["Equal total area does not guarantee a legal arrangement. Every domino must cover one square of each color, so the color imbalance applies to every possible placement. A reason that applies to all placements settles the question without trying each placement individually."]}, {"id": 209, "a": ["Participants received a **blank board**, a **colored board**, a board labeled **black and pink**, or one labeled **bread and butter**. The underlying covering problem stayed a pairing problem across two kinds of square in every condition, and the labels changed how easily that pairing could be represented."]}, {"id": 210, "a": ["Participants in the **bread-and-butter** condition solved the problem **twice as fast** as those in the blank condition. The labels emphasized the difference between the two square types and their pairing, which made it easier to represent each domino as needing one of each kind."]}, {"id": 211, "a": ["A **think-aloud protocol** asks a person to say aloud what they are thinking while solving a problem. Participants verbalize new thoughts rather than simply describing their actions, so the record shows which information is receiving attention during the attempt."]}, {"id": 212, "a": ["In the bread-and-butter version, participants can solve the problem once they recognize that the words bread and butter are relevant to the required pairing. Their thinking moves from treating squares as positions to considering the relationship between the two kinds of square, which is a representational shift related to restructuring. Verbal reports can show when that pairing enters the person’s reasoning."]}, {"id": 213, "a": ["The **Gestalt approach** emphasizes how a problem is represented and restructured, with **insight** as a sudden realization. **Newell and Simon** describe problem solving as searching a problem space of states, using operators to move between them. One asks how the person organizes the problem, and the other asks how a route through the possible states is found."]}, {"id": 214, "a": ["In Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), warmth for algebra rose gradually, while warmth for insight problems stayed low and then jumped sharply. So insight does not come with a steady feeling of getting closer, whereas methodical solving does, because successive operations provide information about progress."]}, {"id": 215, "a": ["**Restructuring** is the change in representation, and **insight** is the sudden realization that follows when a crucial element is found in the new representation. Restructuring describes what changes in how the problem is organized, and insight describes how the solution feels to the solver."]}, {"id": 216, "a": ["**Functional fixedness** restricts an object to its familiar function, while a **mental set** is a preconceived approach to solving based on past experience. Both block solutions, but one concerns objects and the other concerns methods."]}, {"id": 217, "a": ["In the **candle problem** and the **two-string problem**, an everyday object has a familiar function that blocks seeing its useful property. The matchbox’s familiar job is holding matches, which hides its use as a platform, and the pliers’ familiar job is gripping, which hides their use as a weight."]}, {"id": 218, "a": ["In the two-string problem, the hanging string changes from something to be reached where it hangs into something that can be made to swing within reach. The representation changes, which is what restructuring describes, and the hint made the string’s potential use as a pendulum available."]}, {"id": 219, "a": ["Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the candle problem, and participants given bread-and-butter labels solved the checkerboard twice as fast as the blank condition. In each case the presentation made a useful relationship easier to represent."]}, {"id": 220, "a": ["Repeated success with B − A − 2C builds a mental set, and the simpler A + C or A − C solutions can be missed even though the familiar method still works. The method that experience makes available comes first, and that is what can inhibit the simpler solution."]}, {"id": 221, "a": ["Operators define the legal moves that change one state into another, and search is finding a sequence of those moves from the initial state through the intermediate states to the goal. Together they describe a problem space: the states, the rules for moving between them, and the route found by searching."]}, {"id": 222, "a": ["In the Tower of Hanoi, the rules limit which disks can move at any step, and in the tile puzzle the empty space can exchange only with an adjacent tile. Both have an initial state, a goal state, intermediate states and rules for legal moves, and solving means choosing a route through those states."]}, {"id": 223, "a": ["A subgoal makes a later required move legal, such as freeing the large disk, even though the temporary arrangement is not the goal itself. It is a condition that makes part of the larger task possible rather than the complete answer."]}, {"id": 224, "a": ["Means–end analysis organizes the search around reducing the difference between the current state and the goal, using subgoals to do so. It is a way of carrying out the search through a problem space that Newell and Simon described."]}, {"id": 225, "a": ["The way a problem is represented, including its wording and labels, changes which relationships are noticed and therefore how hard the problem is. Restructuring is what happens when the representation changes so that a useful relationship becomes available."]}, {"id": 226, "a": ["The color pairing of every domino and the two removed corners of the same color show that the covering is impossible without trying individual placements. The constraint applies to every possible arrangement, so one general reason settles the question."]}, {"id": 227, "a": ["The bread-and-butter labels pointed toward the pairing of two kinds of square, which changed how the squares were represented. This matches the Gestalt idea that a shift in representation can make a solution available, while the objects and the covering requirement stayed the same."]}, {"id": 228, "a": ["Verbal reports show when the pairing of the two labels enters the person’s reasoning. The shift from squares as positions to a relationship between two kinds of square can be observed during the attempt, which makes the representational shift visible."]}, {"id": 229, "a": ["The triangle and chain problems are solved by a change in representation, such as treating a whole section of chain as connecting links. **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** classed them as insight problems and compared them with algebra, which has a more methodical route."]}, {"id": 230, "a": ["Fixation, functional fixedness and mental set are obstacles that come from how the problem is represented or approached. The 64-disk Tower of Hanoi shows a different kind of difficulty, because the number of required moves can be very large even when each move is simple."]}, {"id": 231, "a": ["Each problem has an obstacle between a present state and a goal, and the solution is not immediately obvious. The solution is found either by restructuring the representation or by searching for a route through states."]}, {"id": 232, "a": ["Insight is a sudden realization that follows restructuring, and in Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) warmth for insight problems stayed low and then jumped sharply. Noninsight problems such as algebra allow a more methodical process, and warmth rose gradually along the way."]}, {"id": 233, "a": ["**Reorganization and insight** concern how a problem is represented in the mind. A person can organize the elements in a way that hides a useful relationship, and reorganizing the representation can make the solution available, sometimes with the sudden realization associated with insight."]}, {"id": 234, "a": ["**Problem solving as search** is the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution. The solver has to find a route through possible states, and specifying the starting conditions and the goal does not by itself identify the sequence that connects them."]}, {"id": 235, "a": ["How a problem is stated affects its difficulty, because the presentation influences the relationships a person represents. In the mutilated checkerboard, total area hides a constraint that becomes visible when you think about the pairing of different-colored squares, so the color relationship supplies what the area calculation cannot."]}, {"id": 236, "a": ["The **Russian marriage problem** begins with 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women, and a matchmaker arranges 32 heterosexual marriages, one person from each group per marriage. Two bachelors then die after a contest involving pierogies, leaving 30 men and 32 women. The question is whether rearranging the survivors can produce 31 marriages under the same one-from-each-group rule."]}, {"id": 237, "a": ["The answer is no, because the two groups are no longer equal. At most 30 marriages can be formed, and two women are left without a partner from the other group. Dividing 62 people by two gives 31 pairs by total size, but that ignores which kinds of person each pair must contain, and no rearrangement creates another man."]}, {"id": 238, "a": ["A domino covers two adjacent squares, one of each color. Removing two opposite corner squares removes two squares of the same color, so the remaining board has unequal numbers of the two colors even though its 62 squares match the area of 31 dominoes. This is the same structure as the marriage problem: one member of each kind must be paired."]}, {"id": 239, "a": ["Every complete covering would have to pair each square of one color with a square of the other. Because one color is more numerous, some squares would stay unmatched, and moving the dominoes into a different arrangement cannot change that imbalance."]}, {"id": 240, "a": ["**Analogical problem solving** uses the solution to a similar problem to guide the solution to a new one. The **target** is the problem being solved, and a **source** is another problem that shares relevant similarities with it."]}, {"id": 241, "a": ["The Russian marriage problem can serve as the source for the mutilated checkerboard. Marriages correspond to dominoes, the two groups of people correspond to the two square colors, and removing two bachelors corresponds to removing two same-colored squares. In each case a valid pairing needs one member of each group, and each change leaves an imbalance."]}, {"id": 242, "a": ["**Analogical transfer** is the transfer of a solution from the source problem to the target problem. Learning about the marriage problem helps with the checkerboard because it supplies a way to recognize the one-of-each pairing, and what carries over is the relationship among the elements rather than their identical appearance."]}, {"id": 243, "a": ["In **Duncker’s radiation problem**, a patient has a malignant tumor in the stomach that cannot be operated on, and the tumor must be destroyed for the patient to survive. A ray can destroy the tumor only if sufficiently high intensity reaches it at once."]}, {"id": 244, "a": ["In Duncker’s radiation problem, a high-intensity ray would also destroy the healthy tissue it passes through on the way to the tumor. A low-intensity ray would spare that tissue but would not destroy the tumor, so no single intensity satisfies both requirements."]}, {"id": 245, "a": ["In Duncker’s radiation problem, the solution is to send **several low-intensity rays converging on the tumor from different directions at the same time**. Each path through the surrounding tissue receives only a low intensity, while the combined intensity at the tumor is enough to destroy it. Only about 10 percent of participants reach this without the helpful analogy."]}, {"id": 246, "a": ["In the **fortress problem**, a dictator rules from a strong fortress, and a rebel general has an army strong enough to capture it if the whole force attacks. Mines on each road detonate when a large force travels together, so sending the entire army down one road would destroy the road and damage nearby villages, while a small group can pass safely but is too weak to take the fortress."]}, {"id": 247, "a": ["The general divides the army into small groups that approach along different roads, each staying below the danger threshold. If the groups arrive together, their combined force is enough for the attack, which satisfies the limit on each road and the total force the fortress requires."]}, {"id": 248, "a": ["The fortress corresponds to the tumor, the small groups correspond to low-intensity rays, and the roads correspond to paths through the surrounding tissue. Sending one large force down one road corresponds to sending one damaging high-intensity ray through healthy tissue. In both, convergence keeps a strong effect at the destination while limiting what travels along each approach."]}, {"id": 249, "a": ["Timing matters because the separate contributions must combine at the target. Dividing the army, or simply lowering every ray’s intensity, without combining the effects leaves the target requirement unmet."]}, {"id": 250, "a": ["**Gick and Holyoak** distinguished three steps. **Noticing** is recognizing that a source problem may be relevant to the current target, and it is the crucial step and often the hardest. **Mapping** connects the elements and relationships of the source to those of the target, and **applying** uses those correspondences to generate a parallel solution."]}, {"id": 251, "a": ["In Gick and Holyoak’s study, after reading the fortress story **about 30 percent of participants solved the radiation problem**. Many had encountered the source without recognizing how it applied to the medical problem, so having the story in memory did not guarantee that they would use its solution."]}, {"id": 252, "a": ["In Gick and Holyoak’s study, when participants were told to think about the story they had read, **success rose to about 75 percent**. The reminder helped make the relevant source available for mapping and application, which separates encountering a useful analogy from noticing and using it."]}, {"id": 253, "a": ["**Surface features** are the specific elements of a problem, such as a fortress, an army, a tumor or a ray. They help describe the situation, but two problems can use very different objects while sharing the relationship needed for a solution."]}, {"id": 254, "a": ["A fortress and a tumor do not look alike, so focusing on that difference can keep a person from noticing that both tasks need a strong combined effect at a central target while the separate approaches stay safe. Making the surface features more similar makes the shared relationship easier to notice, because noticing finds a possible source and mapping works out how it corresponds to the target."]}, {"id": 255, "a": ["In the **lightbulb problem**, Ruth finds an expensive laboratory bulb with a broken filament inside sealed glass. The bulb must still produce precisely controlled light, and she cannot open it, so the two broken parts of the filament must be fused without breaking the surrounding glass. A high-intensity laser would fuse the filament but also break the fragile glass, and a low-intensity beam would preserve the glass without fusing the filament."]}, {"id": 256, "a": ["The solution is to direct **several low-intensity laser beams at the filament from different directions at once**. Their combined effect fuses the filament, while each part of the glass receives only a low-intensity beam. The filament needs a strong local effect and the glass limits the intensity along any single path, so separating the paths and combining their effects meets both requirements."]}, {"id": 257, "a": ["**About 81 percent** of participants who knew Duncker’s radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with **about 10 percent** in the control group. The two tasks share relatively similar surface features, since both direct a beam toward an internal target, which makes the connection easier to notice than the connection to the army and fortress."]}, {"id": 258, "a": ["The two tasks also share relatively similar surface features, since both direct a beam toward an internal target. That similarity makes a possible connection easier to notice than comparing medical radiation with an army approaching a fortress."]}, {"id": 259, "a": ["**Structural features** are the underlying principle that governs the solution. In the radiation problem a strong ray destroys healthy tissue on its way to the tumor, and in the fragile-glass lightbulb problem a strong laser breaks the glass on its way to the filament. In both, enough intensity must reach an internal target while the intensity delivered through its surroundings stays restricted, and that is why several low-intensity approaches must converge."]}, {"id": 260, "a": ["Two versions of the lightbulb problem can keep the same surface features, the same bulb, filament and lasers, while changing the structure. Changing why a single laser fails changes the relationship between the source and the radiation target, even though the objects stay the same."]}, {"id": 261, "a": ["In the **fragile-glass** source, a high-intensity beam would fuse the filament but also break the surrounding glass, and a lower intensity would protect the glass but fail to repair the filament. Ruth places several lasers around the bulb and directs their low-intensity beams at the filament together. The radiation target has the same structure, since healthy tissue limits any single ray, while the tumor needs a stronger combined intensity."]}, {"id": 262, "a": ["About 69 percent of participants who read the fragile-glass solution solved the radiation problem. The source supplies both the converging-beam procedure and the constraint that explains why the procedure is needed, so the useful correspondence is between a strong target effect and protected surroundings."]}, {"id": 263, "a": ["In the **insufficient-intensity** source, the laser can only produce low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament. Ruth again directs several lasers at the filament together, and their combined effect supplies the needed intensity. The problem does not make damage to the surrounding glass the reason a single beam cannot be used."]}, {"id": 264, "a": ["After the insufficient-intensity source about 33 percent of participants solved the radiation problem, compared with about 69 percent after the fragile-glass source. Both sources use converging laser beams, but they give different reasons for combining them, and the different transfer rates accompany that difference in the underlying constraints."]}, {"id": 265, "a": ["**Analogical encoding** is comparing two problems to determine the similarities between them. Comparing draws attention to relationships that appear in both, rather than to each set of surface details on its own, so the structure that governs the solution becomes easier to see."]}, {"id": 266, "a": ["Comparing source stories directs reasoning toward the structural features that govern the solution. A later target can then be approached through the relationship the sources share, instead of depending entirely on the appearance of one familiar situation."]}, {"id": 267, "a": ["The **analogical paradox** is the contrast between how hard analogies are to apply in laboratory tasks and how routinely people use analogies in real-world settings. A person can miss an experimentally supplied source and still use relationships from other situations during everyday problem solving."]}, {"id": 268, "a": ["Scientists use about **3 to 15 analogies in a one-hour laboratory meeting**. Their discussion connects the current question with another situation or a known solution, so analogical reasoning happens within ongoing work and not only when an experiment assigns a source and a target."]}, {"id": 269, "a": ["**In vivo problem-solving research** observes people solving problems in real-world settings, which keeps the ordinary context and its information and interactions. The observations can be time-consuming, and researchers cannot isolate and control variables as they can in a constrained experiment, so the method trades control for ecological realism."]}, {"id": 270, "a": ["An **expert** is a person recognized as extremely knowledgeable or skilled in a field after devoting a large amount of time to learning, practicing and applying that learning. Expertise is tied to substantial experience within a domain, not to confidence about a single task."]}, {"id": 271, "a": ["A chess expert can have spent roughly **10,000 to 20,000 hours** studying and playing chess. Experts solve problems within their field faster and with a higher success rate than beginners, and that comparison concerns performance in the area where their knowledge has developed."]}, {"id": 272, "a": ["**Chase and Simon (1973)** compared a chess master with a beginner who had fewer than 100 hours of experience. Each viewed a chessboard for five seconds and then tried to reproduce where the pieces had been, with either actual game positions or random arrangements."]}, {"id": 273, "a": ["In Chase and Simon (1973), the expert did much better than the beginner for actual game positions. Long-term memory made meaningful arrangements available as **chunks**, which group related information into a useful unit. The same chunks were not available for random arrangements, so the expert’s recall reflects organized domain knowledge rather than an equal advantage for every arrangement."]}, {"id": 274, "a": ["How well people can access information when solving depends on knowledge organization. Knowing more facts comes with differences in how relevant problems are grouped and related to the principles needed to solve them."]}, {"id": 275, "a": ["**Chi et al. (1982)** presented 24 physics problems to experts, who were physics professors, and to novices, who were students with one semester of physics. Participants sorted the problems into groups by similarity, so the task concerned how they represented relationships among problems rather than whether they could recite a definition."]}, {"id": 276, "a": ["In Chi et al. (1982), **novices grouped problems by surface features**, such as the appearance of the objects involved. **Experts grouped problems by structural features**, including the underlying principles. Knowledge is therefore accessed through a relationship relevant to solving, not only through a matching object description."]}, {"id": 277, "a": ["In Chi et al. (1982), the novice grouped problems 23 and 24 together because both involve inclined planes. The expert grouped problems 21 and 24 together through conservation of energy, even though problem 21 has a block and spring on a horizontal surface. The same problem 24 therefore belongs with a different partner under the two organizations."]}, {"id": 278, "a": ["Experts spend more time analyzing a problem before they proceed. That slower start can pay off, because examining the problem and identifying its relevant relationships lets them solve the overall problem efficiently."]}, {"id": 279, "a": ["The advantage is specific to the expert’s field. Experts are no better than novices on problems outside it, because the knowledge and organization built through practice do not automatically supply the relationships an unrelated problem needs."]}, {"id": 280, "a": ["Expertise can make a person less open to new ways of looking at a problem. A highly familiar approach can remain the preferred interpretation even when the task calls for another, and some problems require rejecting usual procedures in favor of less frequently used ones."]}, {"id": 281, "a": ["**Creativity** includes innovative thinking, novel ideas and new connections between existing ideas. **Divergent thinking** is open-ended and allows many potential solutions, so the activity is about generating possibilities rather than settling on one immediate response."]}, {"id": 282, "a": ["Creative problem solving usually involves more than getting an idea. It can include a lengthy period of trial and error, as possibilities are developed and tested, and the idea becomes part of a process that also settles what the problem is and how a response can be carried out."]}, {"id": 283, "a": ["Creative problem solving has four parts. **Problem generation** includes problem finding and fact finding. **Problem formulation** includes defining the problem and finding ideas. **Problem solving** includes evaluation and planning. **Solution implementation** means taking action."]}, {"id": 284, "a": ["Generating ideas is one part of the process, and without ideas it stops, because there is no proposed approach to develop. Producing an idea does not complete the evaluation, planning and action needed to carry a solution out."]}, {"id": 285, "a": ["Too much knowledge can sometimes hinder creative problem solving. When familiar approaches constrain which possibilities are considered, an accepted procedure can dominate the representation even when the problem requires a different relationship."]}, {"id": 286, "a": ["**Flexible thinking** includes being willing to reject an accepted procedure when it does not fit the problem. Knowledge remains available, but the solver has to reconsider how it is being used, rather than relying rigidly on an established way of proceeding."]}, {"id": 287, "a": ["The Russian marriage problem is the source and the mutilated checkerboard is the target. Marriages map to dominoes and the two groups to the two colors, so recognizing the one-of-each pairing transfers the solution from one problem to the other."]}, {"id": 288, "a": ["**Noticing** finds a relevant source, **mapping** connects its elements to the target, and **applying** generates the parallel solution. Gick and Holyoak’s results show that success can fail at noticing even when the source is known, since about 30 percent solved the radiation problem after the fortress story."]}, {"id": 289, "a": ["In Duncker’s radiation problem, the fortress and the radiation problem both require enough force at a central target while limiting what travels along each approach. Each is solved by combining weak approaches into a strong effect at the destination, with the fortress corresponding to the tumor, the small groups to low-intensity rays and the roads to paths through tissue."]}, {"id": 290, "a": ["A reminder to think about the story raised success in Gick and Holyoak’s study from about 30 to about 75 percent, so noticing the relevance of a source matters a great deal. The radiation source gave about 81 percent on the lightbulb problem, so similar surface features also help people notice the connection."]}, {"id": 291, "a": ["Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice, as in radiation to lightbulb. Structural features, the constraints that make convergence useful, decide whether the solution actually applies, so surface features help you find the source and structure is what makes it useful."]}, {"id": 292, "a": ["About 69 percent solved the radiation problem after the fragile-glass source, which shares the surrounding-damage constraint with the radiation problem. About 33 percent did after the insufficient-intensity source, which gives a different reason for converging beams. The different rates accompany a difference in the underlying constraints."]}, {"id": 293, "a": ["Comparing sources draws attention to shared structure, which helps people notice and map relationships they would not use on their own. This addresses the difficulty Gick and Holyoak found, where many participants did not apply a source they had read."]}, {"id": 294, "a": ["The contrast between laboratory difficulty and everyday use of analogies is the analogical paradox. In vivo research studies analogies in real settings, where scientists use many in a single meeting, at the cost of the experimental control a laboratory provides."]}, {"id": 295, "a": ["Chase and Simon (1973) and Chi et al. (1982) both show that expertise organizes knowledge around meaningful relationships. For chess, these are chunks of piece positions, and in physics they are structural principles. In Chase and Simon the expert recalls meaningful arrangements, and in Chi et al. experts group problems by their underlying principles."]}, {"id": 296, "a": ["Chase and Simon (1973) measured memory for chess positions, comparing meaningful game arrangements with random ones. Chi et al. (1982) measured how problems are grouped, with novices sorting by surface features and experts by structural features."]}, {"id": 297, "a": ["In Chase and Simon (1973), the expert’s advantage comes from familiar chunks. Randomizing the pieces removes the relationships that experience makes useful, so the expert has no chunks to draw on and the advantage largely disappears."]}, {"id": 298, "a": ["Novices’ surface grouping in Chi et al. (1982) and the failures of analogical transfer in Gick and Holyoak both reflect attention to surface features, whether similar-looking objects or different-looking scenarios. What is missed is the structural principle that governs the solution, which is the thing experts, grouping by principle, tend to use."]}, {"id": 299, "a": ["Experts’ organized knowledge helps within their field, so the extra analysis pays off there. Outside the field the same organization does not supply the relationships needed, which is why experts are no better than novices on unrelated problems."]}, {"id": 300, "a": ["An expert’s accepted procedure and a familiar approach that constrains which possibilities are considered both involve a familiar approach dominating. Either can block a better approach, which is the same risk the earlier discussion of mental set described."]}, {"id": 301, "a": ["In an analogy, the relationship among the elements of a source is used to recognize the relationship in the target. That can make a solution available, in the same way that reorganizing a representation can."]}, {"id": 302, "a": ["Divergent thinking generates many potential solutions, and formulation includes finding ideas. Evaluation, planning and implementation are separate parts that carry an idea through to an outcome."]}, {"id": 303, "a": ["Knowledge helps when it is organized around structure and hurts when a familiar approach dominates, so flexible use and noticing matter both in analogy and in creative problem solving."]}, {"id": 304, "a": ["Problem generation includes finding the problem and its facts, formulation defines the problem and finds ideas, and solving includes evaluation and planning. Solution implementation then takes action. The earlier parts establish what needs to be addressed, and the later parts carry out a response."]}, {"id": 305, "a": ["The pairing of two groups explains why neither the 62 survivors nor the 62 squares can be covered in pairs. One representation solves two problems that look different on the surface."]}, {"id": 306, "a": ["Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice, and structural features, the constraints that make a solution useful, decide whether it applies. Transfer was higher after the fragile-glass source than after the insufficient-intensity source, and the different rates accompany a difference in the underlying constraints."]}, {"id": 307, "a": ["**Decisions** are the process of making choices between alternatives, and **reasoning** is the process of drawing conclusions. A choice concerns which alternative to take, while a conclusion concerns what follows from the information available. The two can contribute to the same activity without being identical."]}, {"id": 308, "a": ["Decisions are based on the **judgments** people make. A judgment can feed a decision through an evidence-based inference: the conclusion says what the observations support, and the decision says what to do among the alternatives."]}, {"id": 309, "a": ["**Inductive reasoning** is one of the primary mechanisms in making judgments. It means reasoning from observations to conclusions based on evidence, so a conclusion is supported by what has been observed rather than selected without reference to it."]}, {"id": 310, "a": ["Inductive reasoning is the basis of scientific investigation. Observations are made, data are collected and conclusions are drawn from the evidence. Those conclusions are probably, but not definitely, true, because the observations support an inference and do not guarantee every case it could be applied to."]}, {"id": 311, "a": ["The strength of an inductive argument depends on the **representativeness, number and quality** of the observations. Representativeness is how well the observed cases reflect the group being described, and a conclusion about a whole group is weaker when the cases leave out relevant variation. Quality is how compelling an observation is for the claim, so more observations are not automatically better."]}, {"id": 312, "a": ["Many white swans support an expectation about swan color, but one clearly observed black swan contradicts the claim that every swan is white. A single high-quality observation can carry substantial weight because of what it establishes about that particular claim."]}, {"id": 313, "a": ["Scientific discoveries use induction when observations support hypotheses and general conclusions. In everyday life, people predict what will happen from what happened before, and this draws most on experience in familiar situations."]}, {"id": 314, "a": ["Some inductive reasoning happens automatically, without a deliberate list of observations or an argument. A familiar pattern can support an expectation rapidly, and that automatic use of experience still rests on a relationship between earlier observations and a present inference."]}, {"id": 315, "a": ["**Heuristics** are shortcuts that help people reach conclusions rapidly by using past experience to guide present behavior. They are rules of thumb that are likely to give a correct answer but are not foolproof, because the cue they rely on usually fits the situation and can sometimes point away from the correct judgment."]}, {"id": 316, "a": ["The **availability heuristic** uses how readily examples come to mind, while the **representativeness heuristic** uses how much a case resembles a category. Both allow rapid judgment from a limited basis. They differ in the property that provides the shortcut: remembered accessibility in one case, and similarity to a familiar kind of case in the other."]}, {"id": 317, "a": ["The **availability heuristic** makes events that are more easily remembered seem more probable than events that are harder to remember. The more examples a person can think of, the more probable an event seems. That can be useful when memorable events are frequent, but memorability also depends on properties other than actual probability."]}, {"id": 318, "a": ["**Distinctiveness** makes an event stand out, and **emotional salience** makes it especially noticeable because of its emotional impact. **Frequent encoding or retrieval** means the event has been stored or recalled repeatedly. Each of these can raise availability without making the event any more common."]}, {"id": 319, "a": ["The word judgment asks whether English has more words beginning with r or more words with r as the third letter. Both alternatives concern how often a letter occurs in a particular position, so answering accurately requires comparing the sizes of two sets of words."]}, {"id": 320, "a": ["About 70 percent of respondents choose words beginning with r, yet words with r in the third position are actually three times more common. The popular answer therefore reverses the real frequency relationship."]}, {"id": 321, "a": ["Retrieving words by their first letter is easier than searching for a letter in the third position. Words beginning with r come to mind readily, so that set feels more numerous. The availability heuristic explains the error: respondents judge frequency by the instances they can recall, and the search cue favors one set."]}, {"id": 322, "a": ["The **causes-of-death comparisons** ask which of two causes would be more likely to kill a randomly selected person in the United States during the following year. The pairs include homicide versus appendicitis, drowning versus an auto–train collision, asthma versus botulism, asthma versus a tornado, and appendicitis versus pregnancy. Each concerns relative likelihood, not how frightening a cause sounds."]}, {"id": 323, "a": ["A death from botulism can become front-page news, while deaths from asthma can go virtually unnoticed. That difference in exposure makes examples of the unusual cause more accessible, so a person remembering the news can judge that cause more probable than it really is. The error comes from treating memory accessibility as a direct measure of the chance of dying from a cause."]}, {"id": 324, "a": ["Asthma is more likely than botulism or a tornado, yet many respondents choose the less likely cause. A dramatic event can be highly available in memory even when the more common cause attracts little attention."]}, {"id": 325, "a": ["The **representativeness heuristic** bases a judgment on how much one event resembles another. A person estimates whether A belongs to class B from how well A’s properties resemble those normally associated with B, so similarity supplies the basis for category membership."]}, {"id": 326, "a": ["**Robert** wears glasses, speaks quietly and reads a lot, and after hearing that description many people judge him more likely to be a librarian than a farmer. The description resembles their expectations about a librarian, which makes that occupation seem the better match. But a good resemblance does not settle how likely the occupation is relative to the alternative."]}, {"id": 327, "a": ["The **base rate** is the background prevalence of the categories, and here it is about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries in America. **Base-rate neglect** occurs when the similarity-based response displaces that background information, which is what happens when the description is treated as enough for a probability judgment."]}, {"id": 328, "a": ["In the **lawyer–engineer problem**, a group has 70 lawyers and 30 engineers. With no other information, the chance of picking an engineer is 30 out of 100, and respondents generally use that base rate correctly when it is all they have."]}, {"id": 329, "a": ["When **Jack** is described as conservative, careful and ambitious, and as enjoying home carpentry, sailing and mathematical puzzles, people tend to call him an engineer. The description resembles their expectations about that occupation, even though the group still contains more lawyers. Base rates are used when they are all that is available and dropped once descriptive information arrives."]}, {"id": 330, "a": ["The **conjunction rule** states that the probability of two events occurring together cannot be greater than the probability of either one alone. Every case that satisfies both conditions also satisfies each one, so adding a requirement restricts the set of cases rather than enlarging it."]}, {"id": 331, "a": ["**Linda** is described as outspoken, bright, trained in philosophy and concerned with social justice. About 82 percent choose Linda is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement over Linda is a bank teller. The added detail makes the answer resemble her description, but feminist bank tellers form a subset of bank tellers, so the conjunction cannot be more probable."]}, {"id": 332, "a": ["In the **hospital problem**, a larger hospital records about 45 births a day and a smaller one about 15, with about half of all babies boys. The question asks which hospital records more days when over 60 percent of the babies are boys, and the answer options are the larger hospital, the smaller hospital or about the same."]}, {"id": 333, "a": ["The most common answer, chosen by 56 percent, is that the two hospitals record about the same. That answer treats the shared overall proportion as enough to decide how often each hospital exceeds the threshold, and it ignores the difference between observing a proportion in 15 births and in 45 births."]}, {"id": 334, "a": ["The **smaller hospital** is the correct answer. Its daily sample is about 15 births rather than 45, and smaller samples vary more from one observation to another, so an extreme proportion is more likely in the smaller group. The larger hospital’s daily proportion is more stable because it includes more births."]}, {"id": 335, "a": ["Choosing the larger hospital because it has more babies confuses an absolute count with the frequency of an extreme proportion. The question asks which hospital more often crosses the 60 percent threshold, not which produces more boys in total."]}, {"id": 336, "a": ["The **law of large numbers** states that as more individuals are randomly drawn from a population, the group becomes more representative of that population. A larger random sample is more likely to reflect the population pattern, because the influence of short runs on the overall result shrinks as the number grows."]}, {"id": 337, "a": ["People often assume that small samples are representative too, expecting a small group to resemble the population simply because it was drawn from it. That expectation understates how much variation is possible when few observations are involved, and it leads to errors in judging a small sample’s likely composition."]}, {"id": 338, "a": ["An **illusory correlation** is a relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is much weaker than assumed. The person perceives two things as strongly related without evidence supporting that degree of association, and an expectation about the relationship can shape which observations get noticed."]}, {"id": 339, "a": ["**Stereotypes** are oversimplified generalizations about a group, often emphasizing negative characteristics. A stereotype directs attention toward behavior that fits the expected description, so those behaviors stand out while other behavior counts for less in the impression formed.", "Selective attention can create an apparent link between a group and a stereotyped behavior. Remembering the matching instances reinforces the expectation, so the apparent evidence is shaped by what was noticed and retained."]}, {"id": 340, "a": ["Stereotype-consistent behaviors are easier to bring to mind because they received particular attention. That accessibility is mistaken for evidence that the behavior is strongly associated with the group, which is how an illusory correlation links to the availability heuristic."]}, {"id": 341, "a": ["**Lord and colleagues (1979)** had people who favored capital punishment and people who opposed it read the same article. Because the participants began with different opinions but evaluated the same material, their judgments concerned the same information approached from opposing positions."]}, {"id": 342, "a": ["In Lord and colleagues (1979), those in favor of capital punishment read the article as favorable to their position, and those against read it as favorable to theirs. Existing attitudes shaped how the evidence was evaluated, and participants focused on information that agreed with their beliefs while disregarding the rest."]}, {"id": 343, "a": ["**Myside bias** is the tendency to generate and evaluate evidence, and to test hypotheses, in ways that favor one’s own opinions and attitudes. The person gives greater weight to information that agrees with the position already held."]}, {"id": 344, "a": ["**Confirmation bias** is the tendency to seek information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it. Myside bias is one form of this, because evidence is selected or evaluated in a way that favors the account already believed."]}, {"id": 345, "a": ["In the **2, 4, 6 task**, participants are given 2, 4, 6 as a sequence that fits a hidden rule. They write their own sets of three numbers with reasons, get feedback only on whether each set fits the rule, and then propose the rule when confident."]}, {"id": 346, "a": ["The actual rule is any three numbers in increasing order. The most common hypothesis is increasing intervals of two, and testing only sequences that satisfy it gives repeated positive feedback. Those successful tests also fit the broader rule, so confirming cases alone cannot separate the narrow hypothesis from the true one. Searching for contradictions would test the hypothesis’s boundaries."]}, {"id": 347, "a": ["**Utility** refers to outcomes that are desirable because they serve a person’s interests. It expresses how beneficial an outcome is to the decision maker, and desirability gives the outcomes their value, while uncertainty concerns how likely they are."]}, {"id": 348, "a": ["**Expected utility theory** assumes that people are rational and, with all relevant information, choose the alternative with the maximum expected utility. The judgment combines the value of possible outcomes with their chances, rather than choosing by how attractive one outcome sounds. The monetary version uses the maximum expected monetary payoff."]}, {"id": 349, "a": ["People find value in things other than money, and many decisions do not maximize the probability of the best outcome. So people are not utility-maximization machines whose behavior always follows the calculated best monetary prospect, and expected utility does not fully describe what they choose."]}, {"id": 350, "a": ["**Casino gambling** shows that people keep choosing an activity even when they know the odds are strongly against them. Knowing the odds does not automatically remove the choice, so decisions involve considerations beyond the maximum expected monetary payoff."]}, {"id": 351, "a": ["Expected utility theory gives specific procedures for finding the best choice according to expected benefit. It is a consistent basis for comparing alternatives when their outcomes and probabilities are known, and the chosen alternative is the one with the highest relevant expected value."]}, {"id": 352, "a": ["In the **jellybean problem**, participants earn one dollar for each red jellybean they draw, with a chance to earn up to seven dollars. They choose between one bowl with 1 red bean out of 10 and another with 7 red beans out of 100, so the chances are 10 percent and 7 percent."]}, {"id": 353, "a": ["Most participants choose the 7-out-of-100 bowl, even though its chance is worse. The larger number of red beans is attractive, and it draws attention away from the denominator, the total number of possible draws. The decision depends on considerations other than the actual probability of winning."]}, {"id": 354, "a": ["After **September 11**, air travel decreased and driving increased, because some people avoided flying by choosing road travel. The change altered the mode of transportation without removing the risk, and the probability of dying in a car accident is much greater than the probability of dying while flying."]}, {"id": 355, "a": ["Avoiding a feared event can increase exposure to a more likely danger. The reported number of Americans who died on the road while avoiding flying exceeded the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights. The emotional impact of the attacks and the relative probabilities of the two alternatives are different bases for a decision, and comparing both alternatives is what reveals the risk."]}, {"id": 356, "a": ["In the **convoy dilemma**, a convoy of food trucks is traveling to a refugee camp during a famine, and airplanes cannot be used. A second camp holds more refugees. Rerouting the convoy saves 1,000 people at the second camp, but 100 people at the first camp die because the trucks no longer arrive there."]}, {"id": 357, "a": ["Counting lives saved against lives lost gives 100 − 1,000 = −900 for keeping the original route and 1,000 − 100 = 900 for rerouting, so rerouting has the better net outcome. **Ritov and Baron (1999)** found that 63 percent did not choose to reroute. Some would keep the original route even if only one person at the first camp would die, because reluctance to cause that loss can outweigh the larger number saved elsewhere."]}, {"id": 358, "a": ["The **Sacred Value Protection Model** of Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner (2000) proposes that people want to protect what they hold sacred. A sacred value is one whose protection cannot be reduced to an ordinary exchange for material advantage, and sacred values are kept separate from secular values."]}, {"id": 359, "a": ["A **taboo tradeoff** intermingles the sacred and secular domains, so treating a sacred concern as exchangeable for a secular benefit feels threatening and provokes moral outrage. A **tragic tradeoff** compares two sacred values with each other, and people are less morally outraged by it, because either choice protects one sacred concern at the expense of another."]}, {"id": 360, "a": ["In the **taboo scenario**, Robert can save Johnny, a five-year-old who needs a liver transplant that costs the hospital one million dollars, or use that money for equipment and salaries. In the **tragic scenario**, two children need a liver transplant, but only one liver is available."]}, {"id": 361, "a": ["In **Tetlock and colleagues (2000)**, under the taboo tradeoff, not saving Johnny produced much greater outrage than saving him, and choosing other uses for the money received the strongest negative response. Under the tragic tradeoff, outrage was similar whether Johnny was saved or not, which shows an interaction between the type of value conflict and the decision made."]}, {"id": 362, "a": ["**Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)** studied sacred-value tradeoffs in an ongoing political conflict. They interviewed 601 Israeli settlers, 535 Palestinian refugees and 719 Palestinian students, and offered the three types of tradeoff deals as possible solutions."]}, {"id": 363, "a": ["In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), the **taboo deal** asks Palestinians to recognize the sacred and historic right of the Jewish people to Israel, with two states, Israel and a Palestinian state in 99 percent of the West Bank and Gaza. **Taboo+** adds a material incentive, one billion U.S. dollars a year for ten years from Israel. The **symbolic** version adds Israel’s recognition of the historic and legitimate right of Palestinians to their own state, and an apology for wrongs done."]}, {"id": 364, "a": ["**Moral absolutists** treat an issue under dispute as a sacred value, so they consider the compromise unacceptable regardless of how much benefit it could produce. **Non-absolutists** also have strong preferences against compromise, but they do not treat the issues as sacred, and they are not indifferent to the dispute."]}, {"id": 365, "a": ["In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), about 46 percent of the settlers held that part of the Land of Israel should never be given up, however great the benefit. About 54 percent of the Palestinian students treated both the right of return and Jerusalem as sacred values. The sacred issue can differ between groups, but refusing to exchange it for benefits is the common basis for classifying them."]}, {"id": 366, "a": ["For **moral absolutists** in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), adding money in Taboo+ increased anger, disgust and support for violence, because money makes an exchange they already treat as sacred more objectionable. For **non-absolutists**, the same material benefit reduced anger and disgust and lowered support for violence."]}, {"id": 367, "a": ["In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), symbolic recognition was associated with lower anger or disgust and lower support for violence than the basic taboo offer. Acknowledging a group’s claim affects responses differently from offering money, because the symbolic term addresses a value that a material exchange can leave threatened."]}, {"id": 368, "a": ["The findings in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007) distinguish material benefit from symbolic reciprocity. Increasing an economic incentive is not automatically an improvement in a negotiation over sacred concerns. What an offer asks people to exchange, and how it treats their commitments, shapes the emotional and behavioral judgments they report."]}, {"id": 369, "a": ["An **incentive** offers a benefit for agreement, and a **disincentive** imposes a cost for refusing. Economic funds change the benefits of accepting, and economic sanctions change the costs of refusing, so both try to alter the practical consequences attached to the decision."]}, {"id": 370, "a": ["**Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010)** and **Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009)** asked whether people who treat Iran’s nuclear program as a sacred value would accept material benefits or costs in exchange for compromise. The answer depends on whether the program is held as a sacred value, not on the material terms alone."]}, {"id": 371, "a": ["In the study by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), the **sacred-value measure**, based on Baron and Spranca (1997), asks what participants think about Iran giving up its nuclear energy or weapons program. Responses range from saying it definitely should happen, through not objecting, to accepting it only if the benefits are large enough.", "A participant is classed as holding a sacred value if they say giving up the program should not be done no matter how great the consequences. Those willing to decide according to sufficiently large benefits do not meet that criterion, which is what separates the sacred-value group from the rest."]}, {"id": 372, "a": ["In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), the online study involved 1,997 Iranian participants and was conducted in Farsi. About 14 percent for nuclear energy and 13 percent for nuclear weapons gave the sacred-value response, so that group is a subgroup within the participants."]}, {"id": 373, "a": ["In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), the design is 2 × 3: the nuclear program is either energy or weapons, and the deal is Taboo, Taboo+ or Taboo−, giving six conditions."]}, {"id": 374, "a": ["In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), in the **Taboo** agreement, Iran gives up its nuclear program and surrenders its facilities to the United Nations, and Israel gives up the corresponding program and facilities in return. **Taboo+** adds 40 billion dollars in economic funds from the United Nations to Iran. **Taboo−** adds the threat of additional sanctions if Iran refuses the deal."]}, {"id": 375, "a": ["In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), for participants with a sacred value about nuclear energy, approval was lower with the financial incentive in Taboo+ than with the basic taboo deal, and lower with sanctions in Taboo− than with the baseline. Anticipated anger was highest with the incentive and elevated with the sanctions threat, so adding a benefit or threatening a cost did not make the compromise more acceptable."]}, {"id": 376, "a": ["In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), participants without a sacred value had much higher approval and lower anger across the energy conditions, and their ratings varied little between the baseline, incentive and sanctions versions. The large difference in responses concerns whether nuclear energy is treated as sacred, not the terms of the deal."]}, {"id": 377, "a": ["Induction draws a probable conclusion from observed evidence, while heuristics are quick shortcuts that rely on past experience and cues such as availability or similarity. Heuristics are likely to give a correct answer but are not foolproof."]}, {"id": 378, "a": ["The **availability** heuristic substitutes ease of remembering for probability, as in the r-word and causes-of-death judgments. The **representativeness** heuristic substitutes resemblance for probability, which leads to ignoring base rates in Robert and Jack and to the conjunction error in Linda."]}, {"id": 379, "a": ["In Robert and Jack, a description that resembles a category displaces the background frequencies of the categories. This is base-rate neglect: the similarity-based response replaces the category-frequency information."]}, {"id": 380, "a": ["In each case the response follows how well a description fits a category, not the probability relationship. In Linda the relationship neglected is the nested set, since feminist bank tellers lie inside the set of bank tellers. In Jack it is the composition of the group, 70 lawyers and 30 engineers."]}, {"id": 381, "a": ["The conjunction rule concerns nested sets, because adding a condition cannot raise probability. The law of large numbers concerns sample size and representativeness, since a larger random sample is more likely to resemble the population."]}, {"id": 382, "a": ["Answering that the two hospitals are about the same ignores the number of births behind each percentage. People also often assume small samples are representative, yet small samples vary more and produce extreme proportions more often."]}, {"id": 383, "a": ["Stereotype-consistent behaviors get attention and are easier to bring to mind, so that accessibility is mistaken for strong evidence of an association. An illusory correlation therefore depends on which examples are accessible."]}, {"id": 384, "a": ["**Confirmation bias** is the broad tendency to seek fitting information and overlook the contrary, and **myside bias** is one form of it, applied to one’s own opinions and attitudes."]}, {"id": 385, "a": ["In **Lord and colleagues (1979)** the same article was read as favorable to each side. In the **2, 4, 6 task**, testing only sequences that fit the hypothesis gives repeated positive feedback and leaves the narrow hypothesis intact. Both show evidence being used in ways that favor the account already believed."]}, {"id": 386, "a": ["Trying sequences that violate the hypothesis tests its boundaries, while confirming triples leave the narrower belief intact. Searching for possible contradictions is what would reveal that the actual rule is any three numbers in increasing order."]}, {"id": 387, "a": ["Expected utility theory says people choose the maximum expected utility, combining the value of outcomes with their chances. But people value things beyond money and sometimes gamble against the odds, so actual behavior departs from the theory."]}, {"id": 388, "a": ["In the **jellybean problem** the larger number of red beans draws attention away from the denominator. In the **hospital problem** the answer that the two are about the same leaves out how many births lie behind each day’s percentage. In both, part of the information is overlooked, and the judgment follows what stands out."]}, {"id": 389, "a": ["In the **causes-of-death comparisons**, distinctive coverage makes an unusual cause more available than a more likely one. After September 11, the emotional impact of the attacks guided travel choices toward the more probable danger of the road. In both, the decision departs from actual probability."]}, {"id": 390, "a": ["In **Ritov and Baron (1999)**, counting lives saved says reroute, yet 63 percent did not, because reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved. Expected utility combines value and chance and chooses the maximum, so the two ways of evaluating outcomes give different answers."]}, {"id": 391, "a": ["A **taboo tradeoff** compares a sacred value with a secular benefit, while a **tragic tradeoff** compares two sacred values. People are less morally outraged by the tragic kind, because either choice protects one sacred concern."]}, {"id": 392, "a": ["Outrage depended on the outcome only in the **taboo** condition, where not saving Johnny was far worse. In the tragic condition outrage was similar for both outcomes, which is the interaction between the type of value conflict and the decision made within it, as Tetlock and colleagues (2000) found."]}, {"id": 393, "a": ["In the **Johnny scenarios** of Tetlock and colleagues (2000), outrage depended on the type of tradeoff. In the **convoy dilemma** of Ritov and Baron (1999), reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved. In both cases the responses were not a simple numerical comparison."]}, {"id": 394, "a": ["For **absolutists** in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), the issue is sacred, so the basic deal is already a taboo proposal. Adding money in Taboo+ makes an exchange they already treat as sacred more objectionable, and increases anger, disgust and support for violence. For non-absolutists the same change reduces them."]}, {"id": 395, "a": ["**Symbolic recognition** addresses the status of the group’s sacred claim, so it was associated with lower anger and lower support for violence in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007). **Money** engages a material exchange, which is a different kind of offer."]}, {"id": 396, "a": ["Both Ginges and colleagues and Dehghani and colleagues compare a taboo deal with versions that add a material benefit or cost, and both separate participants who hold the issue as sacred from those who do not. Ginges and colleagues added money and symbolic recognition, while Dehghani and colleagues added funds in Taboo+ and sanctions in Taboo−."]}, {"id": 397, "a": ["In **Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)**, incentives and sanctions both try to change the material consequences of a decision. For participants with a sacred value about nuclear energy, approval was lower and anticipated anger higher with the incentive and with sanctions than with the baseline."]}, {"id": 398, "a": ["**Expected utility theory** combines the value of outcomes with their chances, so a larger benefit is predicted to raise the expected utility. For moral absolutists in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), adding money increased anger, disgust and support for violence, so a sacred value is not exchanged for benefit in the way the theory predicts."]}, {"id": 399, "a": ["**Emotions take part in decision making** rather than only getting in its way. People with damage to an area of the prefrontal cortex, who have flattened emotions, have impaired decision making. Flattened emotions are weakened, so the person feels much less than usual, and when emotion is reduced in this way decisions get worse."]}, {"id": 400, "a": ["**Anxious people** tend to avoid decisions that could lead to large negative consequences. This is **risk avoidance**: a choice that carries a chance of a large loss feels too threatening, so the option is passed up even when it might turn out well."]}, {"id": 401, "a": ["**Optimistic people** are more likely to ignore negative information and focus on positive information. Some optimism is useful, but too much can lead to poor decisions, because a person who sets aside the negative information decides without the whole picture."]}, {"id": 402, "a": ["**Expected emotions** are emotions that people predict they will feel about an outcome. Before choosing, a person imagines how a good or bad result would feel, and those predicted feelings guide which option they are willing to take."]}, {"id": 403, "a": ["Expected emotions are one determinant of **risk aversion**, the tendency to avoid risks. A person who expects to feel very bad after a loss is less willing to accept a chance of that loss, so the prediction about feelings, rather than the outcome alone, pushes them toward the safer option."]}, {"id": 404, "a": ["People predict their emotions inaccurately. In particular, they believe a particular loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size, and that belief increases risk aversion. Because a loss is expected to hurt more than an equal gain would help, people turn down risks that would be reasonable if the two were weighed evenly."]}, {"id": 405, "a": ["**Kermer and colleagues (2006)** compared people’s expected emotions with their actual emotions. Participants were given $5 and told that a coin flip would either win them another $5 or lose them $3. They rated their happiness first, and that rating was each person’s baseline."]}, {"id": 406, "a": ["Before the coin flip, Kermer and colleagues’ participants expected that losing $3 would lower their happiness by about 4 points below baseline, and winning $5 would raise it by about 2 points. The expected loss was about twice the expected gain, even though the loss was the smaller amount of money."]}, {"id": 407, "a": ["In Kermer and colleagues (2006), after the flip, the actual drop from losing was only about 1 point, and the actual rise from winning was about 1.2 points. Participants overestimated the negative feeling, since losing hurt far less than they predicted. People have coping mechanisms that get activated, but they do not consider those mechanisms when predicting, and the mechanisms soften the real feeling after a loss."]}, {"id": 408, "a": ["**Incidental emotions** are emotions not specifically related to the decision. They come from a person’s general disposition, from a recent experience, or from the surroundings, such as the weather, and they can change how someone feels while deciding."]}, {"id": 409, "a": ["**Simonsohn (2007)** analyzed university admissions decisions and found that applicants’ academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than on sunny days. The weather had nothing to do with the applicants, but it changed what the decision makers paid most attention to."]}, {"id": 410, "a": ["**Simonsohn (2009)** found that prospective students who visited an academically highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they had visited on a cloudy day. The weather on the day of a visit has nothing to do with the quality of the school, which shows that incidental factors can carry into important decisions."]}, {"id": 411, "a": ["**Lerner and colleagues (2004)** had participants view one of three film clips: a sad clip of a person dying, a disgust clip of a person using a dirty toilet, or a neutral clip of fish at the Great Barrier Reef. Each clip set up a mood before any decision was made."]}, {"id": 412, "a": ["In Lerner and colleagues (2004), participants were then given highlighters and asked for two prices, one for selling them and one for buying them. For selling, the neutral group asked about $4.50, the disgust group about $2.70 and the sad group about $3.00."]}, {"id": 413, "a": ["In Lerner and colleagues (2004), for buying, the neutral group offered about $3.50, the disgust group about $3.00 and the sad group about $4.50. Disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price and raised the buying price above the neutral group. The clips had nothing to do with highlighters, so the price differences came from the mood alone."]}, {"id": 414, "a": ["Decisions depend on the **context** in which they are made. Physicians read a hypothetical cesarean case that was always the same, but what came before it changed. In the control context the case came first. In the serious context it followed four cases with serious complications, and in the not serious context it followed four fairly routine cases. Significantly more doctors recommended cesarean after the not serious cases, so the cases just read changed how the same patient was judged."]}, {"id": 415, "a": ["**Opt-in** procedures require a person to take an active step to be an organ donor, and countries with opt-in procedures have consent rates below 28 percent. **Opt-out** procedures make a person a donor unless they request not to be, and countries with opt-out procedures have consent rates over 99 percent. The way the choice is presented changes the outcome."]}, {"id": 416, "a": ["**Status quo bias** is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision. Under opt-in, doing nothing leaves a person a non-donor, and under opt-out it leaves them a donor. Since many people do nothing either way, the consent rates end up far apart."]}, {"id": 417, "a": ["In the short story **The International Community**, a chicken, a duck, a turkey, a pheasant, a quail and a partridge travel together to a summit to be asked which sauce they would like to be eaten with. When one bird says it does not want to be eaten at all, the chef says that is out of the question, so the only choice offered is which sauce. It presents a choice of the same kind as the opt-in and opt-out example."]}, {"id": 418, "a": ["The **framing effect** occurs when decisions are influenced by how a choice is stated. The wording can highlight what might be gained or what might be lost, and the facts of the choice stay the same while the statement changes what people focus on."]}, {"id": 419, "a": ["**Tversky and Kahneman (1981)** studied how framing changes the amount of risk people accept. When situations are framed as gains, people tend toward a **risk-aversion strategy** and prefer the sure option. When they are framed as losses, people tend toward a **risk-taking strategy** and prefer the gamble."]}, {"id": 420, "a": ["A **risk-aversion strategy** avoids an uncertain outcome in favor of a certain one. A **risk-taking strategy** accepts an uncertain outcome in the hope of avoiding a certain loss. The same people can switch between the two depending only on whether the outcomes are described as gains or losses."]}, {"id": 421, "a": ["In the **gain frame** of the disease problem, **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)** describe a disease expected to kill 600 people, with two programs proposed. The outcomes are stated as people saved. Program A saves 200 for certain. Program B has a 1/3 chance that 600 are saved and a 2/3 chance that none are saved."]}, {"id": 422, "a": ["In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), in the gain frame, 72 percent chose the sure option, Program A, and 28 percent chose the gamble, Program B. Most people avoided the risk when outcomes were described as lives saved, which fits the risk-aversion strategy the gain frame encourages. Choosing A means giving up the chance to save everyone in exchange for certainty."]}, {"id": 423, "a": ["In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), in the **loss frame**, the same disease is expected to kill 600 people, but the outcomes are stated as people who die. Program C means 400 people die for certain. Program D has a 1/3 chance that no one dies and a 2/3 chance that 600 die."]}, {"id": 424, "a": ["In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), in the loss frame, 78 percent chose the gamble, Program D, and 22 percent chose the sure option, Program C. Program C has the same outcome as Program A, and Program D is the same gamble as Program B, so only the wording changed and most people moved to the gamble. That is the framing effect, and choosing D is a risk-taking strategy taken to avoid a certain loss."]}, {"id": 425, "a": ["**Neuroeconomics** combines psychology, neuroscience and economics. Psychology contributes how people think and decide, neuroscience contributes what happens in the brain, and economics contributes how choices about money and value are analyzed. One finding is that decisions are influenced by emotions that are associated with activity in specific brain areas."]}, {"id": 426, "a": ["In the **ultimatum game** used by **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, a proposer gets $10 and offers a split to a responder. The responder can accept, and the money is divided as offered, or reject, in which case neither player gets anything. A low offer leaves the responder choosing between a small amount and nothing at all."]}, {"id": 427, "a": ["According to utility theory, the rational decision for the responder is to accept any offer, because even $1 is worth more than the nothing that comes from rejecting. The way real responders behave can then be compared with that standard."]}, {"id": 428, "a": ["In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), participants played 20 games, 10 with human partners and 10 with computer partners. Offers of $5 and $5 and of $7 and $3 were accepted almost every time, at about 100 percent and about 95 percent for both kinds of partner.", "Lower offers separated the two kinds of partner. In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), an $8 and $2 offer was accepted about 53 percent of the time from a human and about 85 percent from a computer, and a $9 and $1 offer was accepted about 37 percent from a human and about 68 percent from a computer."]}, {"id": 429, "a": ["In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), participants often rejected low offers because they became angry that the offers were unfair, and they were less angry at an unfair computer. That is why the same low offer was accepted more often when a computer made it, since anger at unfairness is what drives rejection."]}, {"id": 430, "a": ["In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), participants with more activation of the **right anterior insula**, an area connected with emotional states, rejected more offers. Rejecting meant getting nothing, so the emotion linked to this area led people to give up money, which shows that emotion matters in decision making."]}, {"id": 431, "a": ["**Deductive reasoning** is determining whether a conclusion logically follows from its premises. A premise is a statement the reasoning starts from, and deductive reasoning is concerned with whether the conclusion follows from those premises."]}, {"id": 432, "a": ["A **syllogism**, introduced by Aristotle, has two premises and a conclusion. A **categorical syllogism** describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some."]}, {"id": 433, "a": ["In this syllogism, the premises are All birds are animals and All animals eat food, and the conclusion is Therefore, all birds eat food. Birds fall inside the category of animals, and animals fall inside the category of things that eat food, so birds fall inside that category too."]}, {"id": 434, "a": ["A syllogism is **valid** if its conclusion follows logically from its two premises. If the premises of a valid syllogism are true, the conclusion must be true. Validity depends on the form of the reasoning, while truth depends on whether the statements match the world."]}, {"id": 435, "a": ["Validity is not truth. All birds are animals, all animals have four legs, therefore all birds have four legs is valid, because it has the same form as the earlier syllogism. Its conclusion is false only because the second premise is false, so a valid form with a false premise can still produce a false conclusion."]}, {"id": 436, "a": ["A syllogism can be invalid even when every statement sounds reasonable. In All of the students are tired, some tired people are irritable, therefore some of the students are irritable, the conclusion does not follow from the premises, even though each statement seems plausible."]}, {"id": 437, "a": ["**Belief bias** is the tendency to think that a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable. In the study, **71 percent of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid**. Good reasoning and truth are not the same thing, so a believable conclusion led most people to accept reasoning that did not hold."]}, {"id": 438, "a": ["There is no easy method for deciding whether a syllogism is valid, so one way is to picture the situation the statements describe. Most people solve the **pool table problem** this way, by imagining how the balls are arranged, and that idea is the basis of Johnson-Laird’s proposal that people use mental models."]}, {"id": 439, "a": ["In the **pool table problem**, a black ball is directly above the cue ball, the green ball is to the right of the cue ball, and a red ball lies between them. The speaker moves so that the red ball is between the speaker and the black ball. Picturing the table, the cue ball is to the left of the speaker’s line of sight."]}, {"id": 440, "a": ["In **Johnson-Laird (1999)**, a **mental model** is a specific situation represented in a person’s mind that helps determine whether a syllogism is valid. The person builds a picture of the situation described by the premises and reasons from that picture."]}, {"id": 441, "a": ["The person first creates a model of the situation and generates tentative conclusions that might hold. Next, the person looks for **exceptions** that would falsify the model, meaning a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not. The validity of the syllogism is then decided by whether an exception was found."]}, {"id": 442, "a": ["A conclusion is valid only if no model of the premises can refute it, and refuting a conclusion means showing that it can be false. If even one model fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow, so searching for exceptions is how an invalid conclusion is rejected."]}, {"id": 443, "a": ["**Conditional syllogisms** have two premises and a conclusion, and the first premise has the form **If p, then q**. The letter p is a condition, and q is what follows when that condition is met."]}, {"id": 444, "a": ["All four conditional syllogisms share the same first premise. They differ in the second premise, which states p, not p, q or not q, and in the conclusion drawn from it. Two of the four are valid and two are not."]}, {"id": 445, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 1 is modus ponens**: If I study, I’ll get a good grade; I studied; therefore I’ll get a good grade. Since the condition is met, what follows from it has to follow too. The form is valid, and **97 percent of participants judged it correctly**, making it the easiest of the four to evaluate."]}, {"id": 446, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 2 is modus tollens**: If I study, I’ll get a good grade; I didn’t get a good grade; therefore I didn’t study. If studying always leads to a good grade, then missing the grade means the studying did not happen. It is just as valid as modus ponens, yet **only 60 percent of participants judged it correctly**."]}, {"id": 447, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 3** is If I study, I’ll get a good grade; I got a good grade; therefore I studied. It is not valid, and **only 40 percent judged it correctly**. The first premise says what happens when someone studies, but it does not say that studying is the only way to get a good grade."]}, {"id": 448, "a": ["The same form with different content is If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona; I live in Arizona; therefore I live in Tucson. It is easier to see here that the conclusion does not follow, since a person can live in Arizona without living in Tucson."]}, {"id": 449, "a": ["**Conditional syllogism 4** is If I study, I’ll get a good grade; I didn’t study; therefore I didn’t get a good grade. It is not valid, and **only 40 percent judged it correctly**. The first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study, so a good grade could still come from some other reason."]}, {"id": 450, "a": ["The two valid syllogisms were judged correctly at 97 percent and 60 percent, and the two invalid ones at 40 percent each. Both valid types were judged correctly more often than both invalid types."]}, {"id": 451, "a": ["The **Wason four-card problem** tests the effect of using real-world items in a conditional-reasoning problem. Four cards read E, K, 4 and 7, and the task is to find the minimum number of cards to turn over to test the rule if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other."]}, {"id": 452, "a": ["The correct cards are **E and 7**. E must be turned over. Only 7 percent of participants chose the 7, which is correct, while 46 percent chose the 4, which is an error."]}, {"id": 453, "a": ["A 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule, so turning the 7 is a real test of it. A 4 cannot break the rule whatever is on its back, so turning it over tests nothing."]}, {"id": 454, "a": ["The **falsification principle** states that to test a rule you must look for situations that falsify it, meaning prove it false. Most participants fail to do this, which is why so few chose the 7."]}, {"id": 455, "a": ["With the concrete rule If drinking beer, then 21 years old, and cards reading Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old, **73 percent answered correctly**. The cards to turn over are beer and 16 years old, because someone drinking beer while under 21 is the only case that breaks the rule."]}, {"id": 456, "a": ["A **permission schema** is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. It is a familiar pattern of knowledge about rules that grant permission once a condition is met, such as a drinking age."]}, {"id": 457, "a": ["Permission schemas are used in the concrete versions, because people are familiar with rules of this kind. In the beer version, being 21 is the condition that must be satisfied, and drinking beer is what can then be carried out, so the same logical problem becomes easier in a familiar setting. Familiarity alone does not explain the improvement."]}, {"id": 458, "a": ["In **Kermer and colleagues (2006)**, people expected a loss to hurt about twice as much as an equal gain would help, but the actual drop from losing was only about 1 point. Because expected emotions are one determinant of risk aversion, part of that risk aversion rests on overestimated negative feelings."]}, {"id": 459, "a": ["**Expected emotions** are predictions about an outcome of the decision itself. **Incidental emotions** are not specifically related to the decision and come from things like the weather or a mood from an unrelated clip, so one looks forward from the choice and the other comes from outside it."]}, {"id": 460, "a": ["In **Simonsohn (2007, 2009)**, the weather changed how admissions attributes were weighted and whether visitors enrolled. In **Lerner and colleagues (2004)**, mood from film clips changed buying and selling prices. In neither case was the emotion related to the choice."]}, {"id": 461, "a": ["The same cesarean test case drew different recommendations depending on the cases that came just before it. The same choice drew consent rates above 99 percent or below 28 percent depending on whether the procedure was opt-out or opt-in. In both, decisions depend on more than the options themselves."]}, {"id": 462, "a": ["Many people do nothing either way, so the default determines whether they end up donors. Under opt-in doing nothing leaves a person a non-donor, and under opt-out it leaves them a donor."]}, {"id": 463, "a": ["In **Tversky and Kahneman (1981)**, Programs A and C have the same outcome, and Programs B and D are the same gamble. Seventy-two percent chose the sure option in the gain frame and 78 percent chose the gamble in the loss frame, so only the wording changed."]}, {"id": 464, "a": ["**People expect a loss to have a greater impact than a gain of the same size**, which increases risk aversion. In the **framing effect** of Tversky and Kahneman (1981), gain wording leads to the sure option, a risk-aversion strategy, and loss wording leads to the gamble, a risk-taking strategy that accepts uncertainty to avoid a certain loss."]}, {"id": 465, "a": ["In **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, the ultimatum game shows that utility theory says to accept any offer, yet participants rejected low offers when they were angry about unfairness, giving up money in the process."]}, {"id": 466, "a": ["In **Sanfey and colleagues (2003)**, anger at unfair offers explains why they were rejected more often from humans than from computers. Separately, more activation of the right anterior insula, an area connected with emotional states, went with rejecting more offers."]}, {"id": 467, "a": ["In the **ultimatum game** of Sanfey and colleagues (2003), the emotion linked to right anterior insula activation, together with anger at unfair offers, led participants to give up money by rejecting them. In **Kermer and colleagues (2006)**, people expected losing $3 to hurt about twice as much as winning $5 would help, but overestimated the negative feeling."]}, {"id": 468, "a": ["A syllogism can be valid with true or false premises, and it can be invalid even when every statement seems reasonable. Validity does not guarantee a true conclusion, and a believable conclusion does not guarantee validity."]}, {"id": 469, "a": ["A believable conclusion led **71 percent** to accept invalid syllogisms as valid. The interference comes from believability: good reasoning and truth are different things, so a believable conclusion makes invalid reasoning seem sound."]}, {"id": 470, "a": ["A conclusion is valid only if no model of the premises refutes it, so finding one exception shows that the conclusion does not follow. The mental model approach makes this a matter of searching for a case that could falsify."]}, {"id": 471, "a": ["Both the mental model approach and the falsification principle say that reasoning is tested by looking for a case that could show the conclusion or rule to be false, not by collecting cases that fit."]}, {"id": 472, "a": ["Modus ponens and modus tollens are both valid, because each follows from If p, then q together with its second premise. Modus ponens goes from p to q. Modus tollens goes from not q to not p, reasoning from a result that did not happen back to a condition that did not. That is why it is valid, yet only 60 percent judged it correctly, against 97 percent for modus ponens."]}, {"id": 473, "a": ["Conditional syllogisms 3 and 4 are not valid, and they share one problem. Each treats the condition as the only route to the result. The first premise says what happens when p holds, but it says nothing about other ways q could occur, so q can happen without p, and only 40 percent judged either one correctly."]}, {"id": 474, "a": ["In the abstract Wason problem, the 7 is the falsifying card, since a 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule. Only 7 percent chose the 7 and 46 percent chose the 4, because most participants do not look for cards that could falsify the rule. A familiar permission rule, such as a drinking age, makes the falsifying cards clearer, which is why 73 percent answered the beer version correctly."]}, {"id": 475, "a": ["A permission schema supplies a familiar pattern: if the condition is satisfied, the action is allowed. That makes the concrete version easier than the abstract letters and numbers, because the familiar rule shows which case would break it."]}, {"id": 476, "a": ["A **categorical syllogism** describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some. A **conditional syllogism** has a first premise of the form If p, then q, and its four types differ in the second premise, which states p, not p, q or not q."]}, {"id": 477, "a": ["Emotions such as anger, anxiety or sadness change choices, and reasoning biases such as belief bias reduce logical judgments. Neither follows a purely rational standard, such as accepting any offer in the ultimatum game or judging a syllogism only by its form."]}, {"id": 478, "a": ["The **phonological similarity effect** is that lists of similar-sounding items are recalled worse than lists of different-sounding items, even when the items are only read silently. Because the cost appears without anything being spoken, the effect shows that people recode what they see into sound. Acoustic similarity effect is arguably the better name, since the confusion is about how the items sound."]}, {"id": 479, "a": ["People recode what they see into a speech-based form. A purely visual code would cause mix-ups between look-alikes such as E and F, but the actual mix-ups follow sound. This is the basis of the **phonological loop** in working memory."]}, {"id": 480, "a": ["The loop has a **phonological store** and an **articulatory control process** that converts visual input to speech and refreshes the traces. Articulatory suppression occupies that process, so the conversion and refreshing cannot be done. That is why, with visually presented lists, the similarity cost disappears."]}, {"id": 481, "a": ["A word’s **neighborhood** is the set of words that differ from it by one letter (an orthographic neighborhood) or one sound (a phonological neighborhood). **Cat** has a large neighborhood, because many words are one letter or sound away from it, while **hippopotamus** has a small one."]}, {"id": 482, "a": ["**Roodenrys et al. (2002)** found that large neighborhood words are recalled better in short-term memory tasks. Words with many close neighbors are easier to recall than words with few, so long-term knowledge of words helps short-term recall."]}, {"id": 483, "a": ["Because long-term knowledge of words affects short-term recall, memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity. Two lists with the same short-term demands can produce different spans if their words differ in neighborhood size, so span mixes short-term capacity with long-term word knowledge."]}, {"id": 484, "a": ["In the **Brown–Peterson task**, people study consonants and then do a distractor task, counting backward, that blocks rehearsal. The distractor takes the place of the repetition that would normally keep the letters in mind, and the longer the distraction, the worse the recall."]}, {"id": 485, "a": ["**Peterson and Peterson (1959)** judged interference an unlikely cause, because counting is so unlike remembering letters that the distractor could not have displaced them through competition. They concluded that short-term memory loses information through **decay** unless the information is rehearsed."]}, {"id": 486, "a": ["The conclusion of **Peterson and Peterson (1959)** challenged the view that forgetting is due to interference, a view that followed McGeoch’s (1932) critique. Decay is the researchers’ conclusion, and **Keppel and Underwood (1962)** are cited for an alternative account of the same forgetting."]}, {"id": 487, "a": ["The **modality effect** is better memory for the last one or two items of a list when they are heard than when they are read silently. Lip-read or silently mouthed lists show it too, so the advantage is not limited to lists that are actually heard."]}, {"id": 488, "a": ["The modality effect strongly influenced ideas about sensory memory. The better recall of the final items for heard lists was taken as evidence of a back-up store of raw, unanalyzed sensory input that briefly holds information before it is analyzed."]}, {"id": 489, "a": ["**Neath and Surprenant (2003)** favor the explanation that hearing adds extra information about how the list sounds, which helps the final items. On this account, the advantage comes from the sound information itself rather than from a separate raw sensory store."]}, {"id": 490, "a": ["Early work suggested people can report only a small part of a brief display. **Sperling (1960)** argued that this limit comes from recall, not from perception. The limit lies in reporting what was seen, not in how much was registered."]}, {"id": 491, "a": ["In **Sperling (1960)**, with **partial report**, a cue right after the display lets observers report the cued part almost perfectly. Because the observer cannot know in advance which part will be cued, being able to report any cued part shows that a sensory store briefly held nearly all of the display."]}, {"id": 492, "a": ["The real limit in Sperling’s work is the **time needed to report**. If the cue is delayed, performance falls toward the usual limit, because the sensory store is very short-lived and the information fades before it can be reported."]}, {"id": 493, "a": ["The **position error effect** is that items are usually recalled in the correct position, and when they are not, the errors are systematic: the misplaced item lands in an adjacent position. Errors are therefore not random."]}, {"id": 494, "a": ["Because order errors are not random, percent correct hides something important. Scoring only percent correct treats a nearly correct placement the same as a wildly wrong one, while the pattern of errors shows that people keep approximate position information."]}, {"id": 495, "a": ["People seem to use mental images, but self-reports of imagery are unreliable. It is impossible to tell whether the images differ or only the descriptions, so researchers use tasks that require imagery and vary a critical part of the task."]}, {"id": 496, "a": ["**Shepard and Metzler (1971)** had people judge whether two block shapes were the same or mirror images, with one of them turned. Deciding requires comparing the shapes even though one is turned away from the other."]}, {"id": 497, "a": ["In **Shepard and Metzler (1971)**, people mentally rotated one shape, and the larger the turn, the longer the response, at a constant rate. This suggests that mental images are much like real images, and it influenced theories of mental representation."]}, {"id": 498, "a": ["People judge probability with **heuristics**, which trade accuracy for speed. They are usually good enough but sometimes wrong, and **Tversky and Kahneman (1983)** showed that the estimates they produce often differ greatly from objective probability."]}, {"id": 499, "a": ["The **conjunction fallacy** is judging that A and B together are more likely than A alone or B alone. It is an error because a combination is never more likely than either part: every case of A and B is also a case of A and also a case of B."]}, {"id": 500, "a": ["**Shafir, Smith and Osherson (1990)** found that people sometimes rated a combination of professions or activities as more likely than one of its parts. That is the conjunction fallacy in action, and it points to heuristics rather than probability rules as the basis of such judgments."]}, {"id": 501, "a": ["In the **Wason Selection Task**, the rule If a card has a D on one side, it has a 3 on the other is best tested with **D and 7**, since a 7 with a D on its back would falsify it. Most people choose D and 3, but the 3 can only confirm the rule, so choosing it tests the rule less well."]}, {"id": 502, "a": ["The same logic becomes much easier with a familiar thematic rule. For If you borrow my car, you must fill up the gas tank, the cards to choose are **borrowed car** and **empty gas tank**, because a borrowed car with an empty tank is the case that breaks the rule."]}, {"id": 503, "a": ["**Wason and Shapiro (1971)** compared abstract and thematic rules. People test a rule well when they have relevant experience, and abstract rules force them to rely on logic alone."]}, {"id": 504, "a": ["**Framing effects** occur when the wording or context makes a big difference to a choice, even for situations that are otherwise equivalent. **Kahneman and Tversky (1982)** summarized many of them."]}, {"id": 505, "a": ["In classic framing research by Tversky and Kahneman, the same choice can be presented in two ways. Gain wording typically leads to the sure option and loss wording to the gamble. Choices can therefore follow the framing and not the substance of the situation."]}, {"id": 506, "a": ["The **Monty Hall 3 Door problem** shows poor intuition about the probability of combinations of events. After you pick one of three doors, the host, who always plays fair this way, opens a door with no prize."]}, {"id": 507, "a": ["You should switch, which wins about two-thirds of the time. Your first pick keeps its 1 in 3 chance, so the 2 in 3 held by the other two doors moves to the one remaining door. Many people think stay or switch makes no difference, which is incorrect."]}, {"id": 508, "a": ["The Monty Hall problem shows that intuition about the probability of combinations of events is poor. The feeling that two doors left means a one-in-two chance for each is wrong, and the reasoning that keeps the original 1 in 3 chance favors switching."]}, {"id": 509, "a": ["**Risky choices** depend on how outcomes are framed and perceived. People pick between a guaranteed outcome and a gamble, and the guaranteed outcome can be a gain or a loss, so the same structure of choice can feel very different depending on which it is."]}, {"id": 510, "a": ["In prospect-theory research by Kahneman and Tversky, people typically avoid risk with gains, taking the sure thing, and seek risk with losses, taking the gamble. With a possible gain the guaranteed amount is preferred, and with a possible loss people prefer to gamble over accepting the guaranteed loss."]}, {"id": 511, "a": ["Decisions about risk depend on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses, not only on the objective odds. If only the odds mattered, the same choice would be treated the same way whatever it was called."]}, {"id": 512, "a": ["**German and Defeyter (2000)** tested whether young children get stuck on an object’s usual function, which is **functional fixedness**. They used a box that was first shown as a container, and asked whether having seen that use would make it harder to use the box as a support."]}, {"id": 513, "a": ["In German and Defeyter (2000), older children aged 6 and 7 were slower to use the box as a support after seeing it as a container. The 5-year-olds were not slowed and were even faster, so the younger children showed no fixedness on the container function."]}, {"id": 514, "a": ["In German and Defeyter (2000), younger children seem to have a more fluid idea of function, so one demonstrated use did not lock the object in. Functional fixedness, in this account, develops with age."]}, {"id": 515, "a": ["In **Leist and colleagues (2022)**, the **irrelevant speech effect** is that speech to be ignored impairs serial recall, and the impairment is greater for changing-state speech than for steady-state speech."]}, {"id": 516, "a": ["Leist and colleagues (2022) found that speech hurt verbal recall but not spatial recall, in both children and adults. The pattern was the same across age groups."]}, {"id": 517, "a": ["In Leist and colleagues (2022), the effect is specific to verbal material, which fits phonological interference better than attention-capture or changing-state accounts. If speech simply captured attention, spatial recall would also have suffered, so the specific effect on verbal recall points to interference within the phonological system."]}, {"id": 518, "a": ["**Morrison and Richmond (2020)** studied **cognitive offloading**, which means using actions such as writing things down to reduce mental demands, as described by Risko and Gilbert (2016). Offloading moves some of the work of remembering out of the mind and into an action in the world."]}, {"id": 519, "a": ["In **Morrison and Richmond (2020)**, offloading improved memory, especially at high loads, and people offloaded more as the load grew. Offloading is therefore most helpful, and most used, when the memory task is hardest."]}, {"id": 520, "a": ["Memory ability did not predict who offloaded in Morrison and Richmond (2020). People of different ability levels were not distinguished by how much they offloaded, so offloading may be a compensatory strategy across abilities."]}, {"id": 521, "a": ["The **bat-and-ball problem** (Frederick, 2005) is that a bat and ball cost $1.10 and the bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. The intuitive answer is 10 cents, and the correct answer is 5 cents. The problem is used because the quick answer that comes to mind is wrong."]}, {"id": 522, "a": ["**Kahneman and Frederick (2002)** called the error **unconscious attribute substitution**. **De Neys and colleagues (2013)** found that wrong reasoners were less confident than people on a control version, which suggests some sensitivity to the problem even when the answer is wrong."]}, {"id": 523, "a": ["**Hoover and Healy (2019)** found that wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than. Because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error, they call it a **sensitivity error**."]}, {"id": 524, "a": ["**Giuliani and colleagues (2023)** combined the **framing effect**, a sure option for gains and a risky option for losses, with the **default effect**, which is choosing the preselected option. The study asked how the two work together in risky choice."]}, {"id": 525, "a": ["In Giuliani and colleagues (2023), a risky default raised risk taking, but a sure default did not raise risk aversion. The two effects were additive, so the frame and the default each added its own influence."]}, {"id": 526, "a": ["In Giuliani and colleagues (2023), an exploratory question asked after the choice showed that the belief about the default’s origin went with following or rejecting it. What people believed about where the default came from was related to whether they followed it."]}, {"id": 527, "a": ["**Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)** argue that adolescents reason about risk like adults in the lab, and that the peer context is what raises risk taking. The risk is therefore not due to poorer reasoning on its own but to the presence of peers."]}, {"id": 528, "a": ["**Chein and colleagues (2011)** scanned adolescents during a driving game played alone or watched by friends. Adolescents took more risks when watched, and they showed greater activity in the reward regions of the brain."]}, {"id": 529, "a": ["In Chein and colleagues (2011), the cognitive-control regions did not change with peers. Because control regions stayed the same while reward activity rose, the authors concluded that the effect worked mainly through the reward circuitry."]}, {"id": 530, "a": ["The phonological similarity effect shows a speech-based code in the phonological loop, while the Brown–Peterson task asks how short-term memory loses information when rehearsal is blocked. Both concern short-term memory, but one is about how information is coded and the other about why it is lost."]}, {"id": 531, "a": ["In Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959), the researchers judged interference an unlikely cause, because counting is so unlike remembering letters, and concluded that short-term memory loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed. That challenged the view that forgetting is due to interference, and Keppel and Underwood (1962) are cited for the alternative."]}, {"id": 532, "a": ["The neighborhood size effect of Roodenrys et al. (2002) shows that long-term word knowledge helps short-term recall, so span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity. The similarity effect shows the speech-based code used in short-term memory. One shows a contribution from long-term memory, and the other shows how information is coded in the short-term store."]}, {"id": 533, "a": ["The modality effect influenced ideas about a raw sensory back-up store. Sperling’s (1960) partial report showed that an iconic store briefly holds nearly all of a display. Both concern the short-lived store of sensory information, one through memory for lists and the other through memory for visual displays."]}, {"id": 534, "a": ["Percent correct treats a misplaced item as simply wrong. The position error effect shows that errors land in adjacent positions, so people keep approximate position information, and only the pattern of errors reveals it."]}, {"id": 535, "a": ["In Shepard and Metzler (1971), response time rose at a constant rate with the angle of turn. That is a measurable pattern in behavior, so imagery can be studied without relying on self-reports, and it supports image-like representations."]}, {"id": 536, "a": ["Heuristics trade accuracy for speed, the conjunction fallacy judges a combination as more likely than one of its parts, and framing lets wording change a choice. In each case the judgment follows a shortcut or the wording rather than the objective probability or the substance of the situation."]}, {"id": 537, "a": ["In the Wason Selection Task, people choose D and 3, and the 3 can only confirm the rule. In the Monty Hall problem, many people think stay or switch makes no difference, although switching wins about two-thirds of the time. Each shows poor intuition about what the evidence implies."]}, {"id": 538, "a": ["Wason and Shapiro (1971) compared abstract and thematic rules, and found that with relevant experience people test a rule well, while abstract rules force them to use logic alone. A thematic rule about borrowing a car ties the cards to situations people know, so the cards that could break the rule are easier to see."]}, {"id": 539, "a": ["Both framing and risky decisions show choices depending on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses. The sure option is favored for gains and the gamble for losses."]}, {"id": 540, "a": ["Giuliani and colleagues (2023) combined framing with defaults. A risky default raised risk taking, and the two effects were additive, so the default added its own influence to the influence of the frame."]}, {"id": 541, "a": ["Functional fixedness is getting stuck on an object’s usual function. In German and Defeyter (2000), younger children showed immunity to it, so one demonstrated use did not lock the object in."]}, {"id": 542, "a": ["Irrelevant speech hurt verbal but not spatial recall in Leist and colleagues (2022), and similar-sounding items are recalled worse in the phonological similarity effect. Both depend on speech-based processes, which is why both affect verbal material specifically."]}, {"id": 543, "a": ["In Morrison and Richmond (2020), offloading helped most at high loads and increased as load grew. Ability did not predict who did it, so offloading relates to the memory load a person faces and not to how good their memory is, which fits a compensatory strategy."]}, {"id": 544, "a": ["Heuristics trade accuracy for speed and are usually good enough but sometimes wrong. In the bat-and-ball problem, the intuitive answer is 10 cents and the correct answer is 5 cents. Both show that an answer that feels natural can be wrong."]}, {"id": 545, "a": ["Hoover and Healy (2019) change the explanation of the bat-and-ball error. They found that wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase more than, and because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause, they call the error a sensitivity error. Kahneman and Frederick (2002) had called it unconscious attribute substitution."]}, {"id": 546, "a": ["Adolescents reason like adults in the lab, according to Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013), but when watched by friends they take more risks, with greater reward-region activity, as Chein and colleagues (2011) found. The authors concluded that the effect worked mainly through reward circuitry and not through changed cognitive control, so together the studies locate the effect of peers in the reward system."]}, {"id": 547, "a": ["A risky default raised risk taking in Giuliani and colleagues (2023), and peers raised adolescent risk taking in Chein and colleagues (2011). In each case something about the situation, a preselected option or the presence of friends, shifted risky choice, so context and not only reasoning changes risk."]}, {"id": 548, "a": ["In the conjunction fallacy, people judge A and B together as more likely than A alone, although a combination is never more likely than either part. In the Wason Selection Task, most people choose D and 3, and the 3 can only confirm the rule, while D and 7 would test it. Each departs from a rule of probability or logic, one by ignoring that a combination cannot be more probable than a part, and the other by not looking for a card that could falsify the rule."]}, {"id": 549, "a": ["In the Brown–Peterson task, a counting distractor blocks rehearsal, and longer distraction gave worse recall. In Sperling’s (1960) partial report, delaying the cue made performance fall toward the usual limit, because the store is very short-lived. Both manipulate time or distraction to show how long information lasts, one in short-term memory with rehearsal blocked and the other in a brief sensory store."]}];
